window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["jammu-2014"] = {"id": "jammu-2014", "title": "University of Jammu 2014", "fullTitle": "University of Jammu M.A. Psychology entrance 2014, booklet code CLM-53718-A (real paper)", "sub": "60 real questions · 2014 · no official key, every answer worked out from standard texts · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"general": "General and Cognitive Psychology", "assessment": "Psychological Assessment", "social": "Social Psychology", "personality": "Personality", "physio": "Biopsychology", "dev": "Developmental Psychology", "clinical": "Clinical Psychology and Therapy", "disorders": "Psychopathology", "research": "Research Methods and Statistics"}, "questions": [{"q": "According to Gestalt theory, which one is right?", "options": {"a": "Perceptual organization is a false criterion", "b": "Perceptual organization is natural", "c": "Perceptual organization is acquired", "d": "Perceptual organizations are partially natural and partially acquired"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: Gestalt psychologists held that perceptual organisation is natural, that is, inborn and built into the way the brain works. Gestalt psychology began with Max Wertheimer's (1912) paper on the phi phenomenon, the movement we see when two lights flash one after the other, and was developed by Wolfgang Köhler (1929) and Kurt Koffka (1935). Their slogan was that the whole is different from the sum of its parts. The brain does not wait for experience to teach it how to group sensations; it organises them into wholes at once, by built-in laws: figure and ground, proximity, similarity, closure, good continuation and the master law of Prägnanz (we see the simplest, most complete form the conditions allow). Example: when Riya sees a circle drawn with a few gaps, she sees a whole circle immediately; nobody had to train her in closure. The opposite camp were the empiricists, such as Hermann von Helmholtz (1867), who said we learn to perceive through experience and unconscious inference. Evidence often cited for the inborn side, though not Gestalt work itself, is the visual cliff of Eleanor Gibson and Richard Walk (1960): crawling infants and newborn animals avoid an apparent drop. Option b is right because the Gestalt school treated organisation as a native property of the perceiving brain, not something acquired by learning. Exam trap: option d, partly natural and partly acquired, is the balanced modern view and sounds wiser, but the stem asks what Gestalt theory said, not what today's textbooks conclude.", "topic": "Gestalt nativism in perception", "fig": "Max Wertheimer"}, {"q": "Perceptions are not affected by:", "options": {"a": "Hunger", "b": "Tension and anxiety", "c": "Suggestions", "d": "Behavior"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: behaviour is not one of the factors that shape perception; it is the outcome that perception guides. Perception is selective: out of all the sensations reaching us, personal (internal) factors decide which we notice and how we interpret them. Textbooks list these determinants: needs and motives, emotional states, set (expectation) and suggestion, past experience and values. Needs: David McClelland and John Atkinson (1948) kept navy men without food for 1, 4 or 16 hours and asked them to identify faint pictures on a screen that was actually blank; food-related answers rose with the hours of hunger. Values: Jerome Bruner and Cecile Goodman (1947) found that poor children overestimated the size of coins more than rich children did. Emotion: tension and anxiety make us quicker to spot threats (perceptual vigilance) or slower to recognise upsetting words (perceptual defence). Suggestion and set: what we are told to expect prepares us to see it. Example: told that a new classmate is a thief, Kabir reads the boy's ordinary glance at his bag as shifty. Option d is right because behaviour comes after perception: we act on what we perceive, so behaviour is the product of the process, not one of the listed influences on it. Exam trap: hunger, anxiety and suggestion sound like things outside perception, but they are exactly the textbook determinants. Key note: behaviour and perception do interact, since what we do (moving the eyes, turning the head) changes what we attend to. But in the textbook list of perceptual determinants (needs, emotions, set and suggestion) behaviour is the odd one out, so d stands.", "topic": "Determinants of perception", "fig": ""}, {"q": "When Mohan was one year old, he did not walk properly; reaching to two he moves perfectly. What was the reason?", "options": {"a": "Growth", "b": "Maturation", "c": "Learning", "d": "Practice"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: Mohan walked well at two because of maturation, the natural unfolding of his body and nervous system. Maturation means changes that come from inherited, biologically timed growth of the brain, nerves, bones and muscles. They appear on a genetic timetable and need little or no practice; maturation supplies readiness, and learning builds on it. Arnold Gesell (1929) made the idea famous. With Helen Thompson he used co-twin control: identical twin girls, T and C, so heredity was held constant. Twin T practised stair climbing every day for six weeks from 46 weeks of age. Twin C began only at 53 weeks, yet after two weeks of practice she had caught up. Training given before the body was ready added little. Walking is the textbook example. Most babies walk alone at about 12 to 15 months, but the timing varies. At one year Mohan's leg muscles, balance and brain control were not yet ready; a year later they were, and he walked smoothly without anyone teaching him. Option b is right because the improvement came with age and readiness, not from any training or experience mentioned in the stem. Exam trap: growth and maturation are often confused. Growth is quantitative, an increase in size (height, weight); maturation is the ripening of structures that makes a new function possible. Learning and practice both depend on experience, which is the opposite of maturation.", "topic": "Maturation versus learning", "fig": "Arnold Gesell"}, {"q": "Which of the following paradigms represents classical conditioning?", "options": {"a": "US-CS-UR", "b": "US-CS-CR", "c": "US-UR/CS-CR", "d": "US-UR-CS-CR"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: the shorthand US-UR/CS-CR represents classical conditioning, with the unconditioned stimulus tied to its unconditioned response and the conditioned stimulus tied to its conditioned response. Ivan Pavlov, whose lectures appeared in English as Conditioned Reflexes (1927), found classical conditioning while studying digestion in dogs. Before conditioning, food in the mouth (the unconditioned stimulus, US) produces salivation automatically (the unconditioned response, UR): an unlearned reflex. During conditioning, a neutral stimulus such as a metronome or bell is presented just before the food, many times. After conditioning, the sound alone (now the conditioned stimulus, CS) produces salivation (now the conditioned response, CR). So the paradigm has two links: US to UR, which the animal is born with, and CS to CR, which it learns. The slash in option c separates them: the inborn reflex on one side, the learned connection on the other. Example: Arjun's mother always calls 'Lunch!' just before serving his favourite rajma. Rajma (US) makes him salivate (UR); after some weeks, hearing 'Lunch!' alone (CS) makes his mouth water (CR). Option c is right because it is the only option that keeps each stimulus with its own response. Exam trap: options that string the letters in one line look like a time sequence, but they mix up which response belongs to which stimulus; the US never produces the CR and the CS never produces the UR. Key note: some books draw the paradigm as a three-stage diagram (before, during, after) rather than this shorthand, but only c keeps US with UR and CS with CR, so c stands.", "topic": "Classical conditioning paradigm", "fig": "Ivan Pavlov"}, {"q": "Previous learning that interferes with the recall of present learning is known as:", "options": {"a": "Regressive inhibition", "b": "Retroactive inhibition", "c": "Proactive inhibition", "d": "Progressive inhibition"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: when earlier (previous) learning interferes with the recall of later (present) learning, it is called proactive inhibition, also called proactive interference. Interference theory explains much forgetting: memories compete, and similar material competes most. 'Pro' means forward, so in proactive inhibition old learning acts forward in time and spoils the new. 'Retro' means backward, so in retroactive inhibition new learning acts backward and spoils the old. The laboratory designs show the difference. Proactive: the experimental group learns list A, then list B, and is tested on B; the control group learns only B. Retroactive: the experimental group learns A, then B, and is tested on A; the control group rests instead of learning B. Georg Müller and Alfons Pilzecker (1900) first described retroactive inhibition. Benton Underwood (1957) then reviewed many studies and showed that the more lists people had learned before, the more of a new list they forgot after a day, so proactive interference explains much everyday forgetting. Example: Kabir gets a new mobile number, but when a form asks for it he keeps writing his old one. The old number (previous learning) is blocking the new one: proactive inhibition. Option c is right because the stem describes interference running forward, from previous learning to present learning. Exam trap: the two terms are often reversed. Remember: proactive = old blocks new; retroactive = new blocks old.", "topic": "Proactive and retroactive inhibition", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Motivation could be described as:", "options": {"a": "A modifier of behavior", "b": "An energizer of behavior", "c": "An instinctual response to a need", "d": "An acquired response to a need"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: motivation is best described as an energiser of behaviour, the inner process that arouses, directs and sustains action towards a goal. The word comes from the Latin movere, 'to move'. The usual cycle runs: a need (a lack, such as food) produces a drive (an aroused state, hunger), which energises goal-directed behaviour until the goal is reached and the drive is reduced. Clark Hull (1943) built the energising idea into his drive-reduction theory: reaction potential = drive x habit strength. Habit decides which response is made; drive supplies the energy. If drive is zero, even a strong habit is not performed. Motivation also gives direction (it picks the goal) and persistence (it keeps behaviour going despite obstacles), but energising is the feature textbooks place first: 'motivation energises and directs behaviour'. Example: Riya has known how to solve past papers all year (habit), but only in the month before the entrance does motivation energise her to revise eight hours a day and keep at it. Option b is right because it names the core function of motivation: it arouses and powers behaviour rather than merely changing it. Exam trap: options c and d look scholarly because they mention needs, but they describe kinds of motives (inborn or learned), not what motivation is.", "topic": "Nature of motivation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "According to James-Lange theory of emotions, felt emotions depend on the:", "options": {"a": "Interpretation of bodily arousal", "b": "Activity of higher brain process", "c": "Perception of bodily changes", "d": "Activity of lower brain areas"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: according to the James-Lange theory, the felt emotion depends on our perception of bodily changes. William James (1884), in his paper 'What is an emotion?', and the Danish physician Carl Lange (1885) reached the same idea independently. Common sense says: we see a bear, feel afraid, then tremble and run. James reversed the order: the stimulus triggers bodily changes first (racing heart, trembling, tears), and our perception of those changes is the emotion. In his words, we feel sorry because we cry, angry because we strike, afraid because we tremble. Take away the bodily changes and, for James, nothing is left but a cold judgement. Example: Arjun sees a snake on the path at night; his heart pounds and his legs shake, and sensing that pounding and shaking is his fear. Walter Cannon (1927) attacked the theory: the viscera react too slowly, the same arousal occurs in different emotions, and animals whose visceral feedback was cut still showed emotional behaviour. Later work on facial feedback revived a milder form of the idea. Option c is right because James and Lange made emotion the awareness of the body's response, with no extra step of interpretation. Exam trap: option a, interpretation of bodily arousal, sounds almost the same, but it is the two-factor theory of Stanley Schachter and Jerome Singer (1962), which adds a cognitive label to arousal.", "topic": "James-Lange theory of emotion", "fig": "William James and Carl Lange"}, {"q": "The group factor theory of intelligence was proposed by:", "options": {"a": "Kelley", "b": "Brown", "c": "Spearman", "d": "Thorndike"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: among the names offered, the group factor theory of intelligence was proposed by Truman L. Kelley (1928). Group factor theory says intelligence is made of several group factors, abilities shared by a cluster of tests (all the verbal tests, all the number tests), lying between one general factor and many specific ones. It answered Charles Spearman (1904), whose two-factor theory allowed only a general factor g plus a specific factor s for each test; later analysts found clusters of tests that correlated with each other more than g alone could explain. Kelley, in Crossroads in the Mind of Man, analysed test scores of school children, argued against a single dominant g and identified group factors such as verbal, number and spatial ability, memory and speed. Louis Thurstone (1938) developed the approach most fully with his seven primary mental abilities, and many textbooks name Kelley alongside Thurstone as a founder of group factor theory. Example: Meera scores high on vocabulary and reading tests but only average on arithmetic and spatial puzzles; a group factor theorist says her verbal tests share a verbal factor that her number tests do not. Option a is right because Kelley is a named founder of group factors and the only one listed here. Exam trap: Thurstone is the usual answer; when he is missing, students reach for Spearman because his theory is also a factor theory, but Spearman's factors are general and specific, not group. Key note: the same stem on the PGIBAMS Raipur 2023 paper offers Thurstone and is answered with Thurstone. This paper leaves Thurstone out, so Kelley, the other named founder of group factor theory, stands.", "topic": "Group factor theory of intelligence", "fig": "Truman L. Kelley"}, {"q": "According to Allport the main unit of structure is:", "options": {"a": "Instinct", "b": "Individual trait", "c": "Functional autonomy", "d": "Proprium"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: for Gordon Allport (1937) the main unit of personality structure is the trait, and the trait that really describes a person is the individual trait. In Personality: A Psychological Interpretation, Allport said traits are real structures inside the person (neuropsychic structures) that make many situations functionally equivalent and so produce consistent behaviour. He separated common traits, shared by people of a culture and useful for comparing them, from individual traits, unique to one person. He thought individual traits were the true units, and in 1961 (Pattern and Growth in Personality) he renamed them personal dispositions: cardinal (one ruling passion that colours almost everything, and rare), central (the 5 to 10 traits a friend would write in a reference letter) and secondary (narrow preferences shown only in some situations). Example: Kabir's friends describe him as honest, punctual, witty, stubborn and generous; these are his central dispositions, while his love of filter coffee is a secondary one. Option b is right because, among the options, only 'individual trait' names a unit of structure in Allport's theory. Exam trap: functional autonomy and proprium are also Allport's own terms, so they look right, but one is a principle of motivation and the other is the self. Key note: Allport called the trait, which he later refined as the personal disposition, the basic unit. Since only 'individual trait' is offered, b stands.", "topic": "Allport's trait theory", "fig": "Gordon Allport"}, {"q": "The types of personality were discussed by:", "options": {"a": "Jung", "b": "Freud", "c": "Pavlov", "d": "Adler"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is a: the classic theory of personality types was put forward by Carl Jung (1921). In Psychological Types, Jung described two attitudes, the direction in which psychic energy flows: introversion (towards the inner world of thoughts and feelings) and extraversion (towards people and things). He added four functions: thinking and feeling, which judge (the rational functions), and sensation and intuition, which perceive (the irrational functions). Each attitude combined with a dominant function gives eight types, such as the extraverted thinking type or the introverted feeling type. Jung's scheme became the basis of the Myers-Briggs Type Indicator, built by Katharine Briggs and her daughter Isabel Briggs Myers (published 1962). Example: Riya recharges alone with a book and trusts her hunches (introverted intuitive), while her cousin Arjun loves crowded parties and wants facts he can check (extraverted sensation). A type theory sorts people into separate categories, unlike a trait theory, which places everyone on continuous dimensions. Option a is right because Jung is the theorist whose name textbooks attach to personality types; introversion and extraversion are his terms. Exam trap: Sigmund Freud (1908) wrote on anal character, Ivan Pavlov later sorted dogs' nervous systems into types, and Alfred Adler late in his career named lifestyle types, so all four names look possible. Key note: Freud, Pavlov and Adler each mentioned types in passing, but the classic personality typology in textbooks is Jung's, so a stands.", "topic": "Jung's personality types", "fig": "Carl Jung"}, {"q": "Environmental determinants of personality include all of the following kinds of factors except:", "options": {"a": "Social factor", "b": "Economic factor", "c": "Birth order", "d": "Psychological factor"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: psychological factors are not environmental determinants of personality; they belong to a separate group of determinants inside the person. Textbooks usually sort the determinants of personality into three groups. Biological determinants: heredity, physique, the endocrine glands and the nervous system. Psychological determinants: intelligence, motives, emotions, attitudes and the self-concept. Environmental determinants: the physical environment (climate, geography), social factors (family, parenting, school, peers, neighbourhood), cultural factors (customs, values, religion) and economic factors (income, social class, opportunities). Birth order, a child's position among brothers and sisters, is a family factor and so part of the social environment. Alfred Adler (1927) made it famous: the firstborn is 'dethroned' by the next baby, the second child tends to be competitive, and the youngest may be pampered. Example: Arjun is the eldest of three in a joint family in Lucknow and takes charge of his siblings (birth order, a social and family factor); his family's modest income shapes his ambitions (economic); his own intelligence and self-confidence are psychological determinants, part of him rather than his surroundings. Option d is right because social factors, economic factors and birth order all come from outside the person, while psychological factors come from within. Exam trap: birth order looks biological because it is fixed at birth, so students pick c; but it matters through the way the family treats each child, which makes it environmental. Key note: the option printed 'Poth order' is a misprint for birth order.", "topic": "Determinants of personality", "fig": ""}, {"q": "According to Adler, personality development is generally influenced by reactions to:", "options": {"a": "Sensory stimuli", "b": "Superiority feelings", "c": "All types of strong feelings", "d": "Inferiority feeling"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: for Alfred Adler (1907), personality develops mainly as a reaction to feelings of inferiority. In his Study of Organ Inferiority, Adler began with organ inferiority: a person with a weak organ or body part tries to compensate for it, sometimes so well that the weakness becomes a strength (the stammering Demosthenes became a great orator). After breaking with Sigmund Freud in 1911 he founded Individual Psychology and widened the idea. Every child starts small, weak and dependent on adults, so every human being begins with inferiority feelings. These feelings are normal and are the spur to growth. Personality takes shape through compensation, the lifelong striving for superiority (later called striving for perfection or completion), guided by a fictional final goal, by social interest and by a style of life formed in the first 4 to 5 years. If inferiority feelings become overwhelming, the result is an inferiority complex; if a person overcompensates and boasts or dominates, the result is a superiority complex. Example: Kabir, the smallest boy in his class and teased at cricket, practises bowling every evening and becomes the school's best spinner; his feeling of inferiority drove the effort. Option d is right because inferiority feeling is the universal starting point to which personality is the reaction. Exam trap: superiority appears in Adler's theory too, so b tempts; but superiority is the goal people strive towards, not the feeling they are reacting to.", "topic": "Adler's inferiority and compensation", "fig": "Alfred Adler"}, {"q": "The following is a non-probability sampling:", "options": {"a": "Random sampling", "b": "Stratified sampling", "c": "Purposive sampling", "d": "Cluster sampling"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: purposive sampling is a non-probability method, because the researcher chooses the cases by judgement rather than by chance. Sampling means selecting a part (a sample) of a population to study. The methods fall into two families. In probability sampling every member of the population has a known, non-zero chance of being chosen, and a random process does the choosing, so sampling error can be estimated and results generalised. Its forms are simple random sampling (lottery or random numbers), systematic sampling (every kth name from a list), stratified random sampling (random draws within strata such as sex or class) and cluster sampling (random choice of whole groups such as schools or villages). In non-probability sampling there is no random process, so each member's chance of selection is unknown. Its forms are purposive or judgement sampling (deliberately picking cases judged typical or informative), quota sampling (filling set numbers for each subgroup by convenience), convenience or incidental sampling (whoever is available) and snowball sampling (participants recruit others). Example: Meera, studying burnout among ICU nurses in a Pune hospital, chooses the six nurses she judges most experienced; that is purposive sampling, and her findings cannot be generalised with known error. Option c is right because purposive selection depends on the researcher's judgement, not on chance. Exam trap: stratified and cluster sampling involve the researcher forming groups, so they seem judgement based, but the selection within or among those groups is random.", "topic": "Non-probability sampling", "fig": ""}, {"q": "An attitude scale with five points on it varying from strongly approved to strongly disapproved is known as:", "options": {"a": "Likert type", "b": "Thurstone type", "c": "Bogardus type", "d": "Guttman type"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: a five-point attitude scale running from strongly approve to strongly disapprove is the Likert type. Rensis Likert (1932), in 'A Technique for the Measurement of Attitudes', introduced the method of summated ratings. The respondent reads a set of statements about an attitude object and marks one of five points for each: strongly approve, approve, undecided, disapprove, strongly disapprove (modern versions say agree and disagree). Answers are scored 5 to 1, reversed for unfavourable statements, and the item scores are added to give a total attitude score. Likert's method needs no panel of judges, so it is quicker to build than the scale of Louis Thurstone (1929) and usually just as reliable, which is why it became the most used attitude scale. Example: Arjun's survey on attitudes to mental health includes 'People with depression can recover fully', with five boxes from strongly agree to strongly disagree; Riya's total over 20 such items tells how favourable her attitude is. Option a is right because the five response points with a neutral middle and the summing of scores are the defining features of the Likert scale. Exam trap: Thurstone's scale also uses statements and also has fixed points, but those points belong to the judges' 11-point sorting, not to the respondent, who only ticks the statements he agrees with.", "topic": "Likert attitude scale", "fig": "Rensis Likert"}, {"q": "Which of the following statements is true in behavioural science research?", "options": {"a": "Measurement is indirect", "b": "Measurement always provides only approximate information", "c": "Many errors in measurement exist because we cannot devise appropriate unit of measurement", "d": "All of the above are true"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: all three statements are true of measurement in behavioural science. First, measurement is indirect. Constructs such as intelligence, anxiety or attitude cannot be seen or weighed; we infer them from behaviour, test answers or ratings, through operational definitions. Second, measurement gives only approximate information. Every score contains error. In classical test theory, which grew from the work of Charles Spearman (1904), an observed score equals a true score plus error. The reliability coefficient and the standard error of measurement (SEM) tell us how large the error is: with an SEM of 3, an IQ of 110 is best read as a band of about 104 to 116. Third, many errors arise because we cannot devise a proper unit. Physics has fixed units (the metre, the kilogram) and a true zero. Psychology has neither for most traits: a score of zero on an anxiety scale does not mean no anxiety, and the step from 100 to 110 IQ may not equal the step from 130 to 140. In the scheme of Stanley Smith Stevens (1946), most psychological scores are ordinal, at best interval. Example: Kabir scores 30 and Riya 15 on an anxiety scale; we cannot say Kabir is twice as anxious. Option d is right because each of a, b and c describes a real limitation of psychological measurement. Exam trap: statement b says 'always', and students are taught that absolute words mark a false option; here the absolute is true, since no psychological score is free of error.", "topic": "Nature of psychological measurement", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The t-test is considered to be very useful because:", "options": {"a": "It can be used when the groups are of unequal size", "b": "It ensures correct rejection of hypothesis", "c": "It can be applied even when the samples are not large", "d": "It can be applied even when the samples are not drawn randomly"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: the t-test is valued because it can be applied even when samples are small. A t-test compares means (one sample against a population value, two independent groups, or the same people tested twice). The older large-sample (z) test uses the normal curve and needs either the population standard deviation or a sample big enough (about 30 or more) for the sample standard deviation to be a stable estimate. With small samples that estimate is unstable, and the ratio no longer follows the normal curve. William Sealy Gosset (1908), a chemist at the Guinness brewery in Dublin, had to judge barley and stout from tiny batches. He worked out the exact distribution of this ratio and, because Guinness barred staff from publishing under their own names, signed his Biometrika paper 'Student': hence Student's t. The t distribution is a family of curves, one for each number of degrees of freedom (df). It is bell-shaped like the normal curve but has heavier tails, so larger values are needed for significance: at the .05 level (two-tailed) the critical t with 10 df is 2.23, against 1.96 for z. As df grows, t approaches the normal curve. Example: Meera tests a relaxation technique on 8 students against 8 controls; with so few cases she uses a t-test with 14 df. Option c is right because Gosset designed the t distribution precisely for small samples. Exam trap: option a is technically true, since an independent t-test can handle unequal group sizes, but the z-test can too, so it is not what made the t-test special. Key note: option a is literally true, but textbooks give the use of small samples as the reason the t-test is so valuable, so c stands.", "topic": "t-test and small samples", "fig": "William Sealy Gosset"}, {"q": "In achievement test which type of validity is required?", "options": {"a": "Face validity", "b": "Concurrent validity", "c": "Predictive validity", "d": "Content validity"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: an achievement test above all needs content validity, so that its items fairly represent what was taught. Validity is the extent to which a test measures what it claims to measure. Its main kinds are content, criterion-related (concurrent and predictive) and construct validity; face validity is a non-technical extra. An achievement test measures how much a student has learned of a defined course, so its validity depends on how well its items sample the syllabus and the instructional objectives. Content validity is built in at the planning stage with a blueprint, or table of specifications: a two-way chart of content areas against objectives (knowledge, understanding, application, as in the taxonomy of Benjamin Bloom, 1956), with the number of items in each cell in proportion to the weight of that topic. Subject experts judge it by comparing items with the syllabus; no correlation coefficient is involved. Example: Arjun's Class 10 science teacher spends 40 percent of the year on chemistry, so about 40 percent of the test marks should be chemistry; a paper full of physics questions would lack content validity however reliable it was. Option d is right because an achievement test looks back at a defined body of teaching, and only content validity checks that coverage. Exam trap: face validity is also judged by looking at the items, so students confuse the two; but face validity is how the test appears to the takers, while content validity is a systematic expert check against the syllabus.", "topic": "Content validity of achievement tests", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In which conditions validity depends on reliability?", "options": {"a": "Heterogeneous test", "b": "Homogeneous test", "c": "Both (A) and (B)", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: validity depends on reliability in both heterogeneous and homogeneous tests. Reliability is consistency of scores; validity is accuracy for a stated purpose. A test can be reliable without being valid, but it cannot be valid without being reliable, because an unreliable score is largely random error, and random error correlates with nothing. Formally, a test's validity coefficient cannot exceed the square root of its reliability coefficient (the index of reliability): if reliability is 0.64, validity can be at most 0.80. Charles Spearman's (1904) correction for attenuation shows how much unreliability lowers an observed correlation. A homogeneous test has items that all measure one trait (a 30-item arithmetic speed test), so its internal consistency is high. A heterogeneous test samples several abilities or topics (a general knowledge quiz or a broad achievement battery), so its items correlate only modestly with each other. In both, scores that change from one day to the next cannot predict anything well. Example: Kabir's spelling test (homogeneous) and Riya's general knowledge quiz (heterogeneous) both give very different scores on two Mondays; neither can then predict school marks, whatever their content. Option c is right because the ceiling that reliability places on validity holds for every kind of test. Exam trap: students link reliability only to homogeneous tests, because internal consistency is easiest to see there, and so pick b. Key note: the link is tighter for homogeneous tests. In a heterogeneous test internal consistency can be modest while validity is good, and pushing homogeneity too high can narrow the content and even lower validity (Jane Loevinger's 1954 'attenuation paradox'). But reliability in the sense of stable scores still limits validity in both kinds of test, so c stands.", "topic": "Reliability as a limit on validity", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following approaches to psychology does not consider the individual's conscious experience?", "options": {"a": "Behavioural approach", "b": "Humanistic approach", "c": "Cognitive approach", "d": "Social learning theory"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: the behavioural approach does not consider the individual's conscious experience. John B. Watson (1913), in 'Psychology as the behaviorist views it', declared that psychology should be the objective study of observable behaviour, with the goal of predicting and controlling it. Consciousness and introspection, the method of the structuralist laboratories, were thrown out as unscientific, because no one else can check what a person reports seeing inside his own mind. Behaviour was explained by stimuli, responses and conditioning. B. F. Skinner (1938) carried this on as radical behaviourism: behaviour is explained by its consequences (reinforcement history), and the mind is treated as a 'black box' that adds nothing to the explanation. Example: Meera wants to stop biting her nails. A behaviourist counts how often she bites, finds the situations that trigger it and sets up a reward chart; he does not ask what she feels or thinks while biting. The other approaches all bring experience or mental processes back in. Option a is right because rejecting conscious experience was the founding principle of behaviourism. Exam trap: social learning theory grew out of behaviourism, so students lump the two together; but Albert Bandura (1977) added cognitive processes such as attention, memory and expectation, so it does consider mental experience.", "topic": "Behaviourism and consciousness", "fig": "John B. Watson"}, {"q": "The following is not included in the process of socialization:", "options": {"a": "Social development", "b": "Learning prosocial behavior", "c": "Maturation", "d": "Learning patterns of aggression"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: maturation is not part of socialisation, because it is biological unfolding rather than learning from society. Socialisation is the lifelong process by which a person learns the norms, values, roles, skills and behaviour patterns of his society and makes them his own. Primary socialisation happens in the family; secondary socialisation continues through school, peers, the media, religion and work. It includes social development: learning to make friends, cooperate and take social roles. It includes prosocial behaviour: sharing, helping and comforting, taught through reinforcement and modelling. It also includes patterns of aggression: in the Bobo doll study of Albert Bandura, Dorothea Ross and Sheila Ross (1961), children who watched an adult hit an inflatable doll later copied the same acts, so aggressive styles are learned from social models, and each culture teaches when and how aggression is allowed. Maturation, by contrast, is the unfolding of inherited, biologically timed changes in the body and nervous system, such as teething, walking and puberty; Arnold Gesell (1929) showed its power in his co-twin study of stair climbing. Maturation provides readiness for learning but is not taught by anyone. Example: Riya learns to greet elders with namaste and to share her tiffin (socialisation); her milk teeth falling out at six is maturation. Option c is right because socialisation covers everything learned from society, and maturation is not learned. Exam trap: aggression sounds antisocial, so students think learning aggression cannot be part of socialisation and pick d; but socialisation transmits all of a society's behaviour patterns, aggressive ones included.", "topic": "Socialisation and maturation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A dyad consists of:", "options": {"a": "Three persons", "b": "Two persons", "c": "Ten persons", "d": "Twenty persons"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: a dyad is a group of two persons. The word comes from the Greek for \"two\", and the German sociologist Georg Simmel (1908) made the dyad and the triad (three persons) basic units in his study of how the number of members shapes group life. Option b is right because a dyad is, by definition, the smallest possible group: two people who interact and depend on each other, such as a married couple, two close friends, or a counsellor and client. Simmel noticed two special features. First, a dyad depends on both members: if one leaves, the group ends, so each person feels directly responsible for it. Second, it is intimate, since there is no audience and no third person to take sides. Add a third person and the triad brings new roles: a mediator, a member who gains from the other two quarrelling, or a coalition of two against one. For example, Riya and Kabir are a dyad as a newly married couple; when their daughter is born they become a triad, and their arguments can now draw the child in. Social psychologists use the same idea for dyadic interaction, such as studies of two strangers getting acquainted. Exam trap: dyad (two) is easily swapped with triad (three); remember \"duo\" and \"dual\" for two, \"tri\" for three. Numbers such as ten or twenty describe small groups or classes, which have no special name of this kind.", "topic": "Dyad and triad", "fig": ""}, {"q": "As compared to a democratic leader, the authoritarian leader:", "options": {"a": "Wields more absolute power", "b": "Makes the group goals more clear", "c": "Increase cohesiveness", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: compared with a democratic leader, the authoritarian leader wields more absolute power. The classic evidence is the leadership climates study of Kurt Lewin, Ronald Lippitt and Ralph White (1939). Adults led after-school clubs of ten-year-old boys in three styles. The authoritarian (autocratic) leader made every decision alone and gave orders one step at a time, so the boys rarely knew the larger plan. The democratic leader let the group discuss and choose, and explained the overall goal. The laissez-faire leader left the boys to themselves. Under autocratic leaders the boys worked hard only while watched, stopped when the leader left the room, and became either hostile (with scapegoating of one boy) or apathetic. Democratic groups were friendlier and more \"we\" minded, kept working without the leader, and the boys liked this style best. Laissez-faire groups got little done. Option a is right because power is the defining difference: the autocrat holds authority alone, while the democrat shares it with the group. Imagine Arjun, a project head who sets every deadline and task himself and never consults the team; he holds more absolute power than Meera, who lets her team vote on the plan. Exam trap: autocratic leaders can get high output in the short run, so students assume they also make goals clearer and the group more united; Lewin's findings show the reverse on both counts, which also rules out \"All of the above\".", "topic": "Lewin's leadership styles", "fig": "Kurt Lewin"}, {"q": "Social psychology:", "options": {"a": "Emphasizes upon the individual or small group", "b": "Studies the culture and society", "c": "Is a branch of sociology and anthropology", "d": "All of the above are true"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: social psychology emphasises the individual or small group. The classic definition is that of Gordon Allport (1954): social psychology studies how the thoughts, feelings and behaviour of individuals are influenced by the actual, imagined or implied presence of others. The unit of analysis is the person in a social situation, along with the small, face-to-face groups (a family, a team, a jury) that the person belongs to. Earlier, his brother Floyd Allport (1924) argued that there is no psychology of groups that is not, in the end, a psychology of individuals. So social psychology asks why Kabir speaks freely among friends but stays silent in a lecture hall, or why Meera gives a wrong answer when the classmates before her all give it, as in the conformity studies of Solomon Asch (1951). Option a is right because social psychology is a branch of psychology, and its focus is how one person thinks, feels and acts among others. By contrast, sociology studies society, its institutions and social classes as wholes, and anthropology studies whole cultures, often through long fieldwork. Social psychology borrows from both, and a sociological social psychology tradition (symbolic interactionism) exists, yet the field belongs to psychology. Exam trap: because social psychology talks about groups, culture and society, students pick \"studies the culture and society\" or call it a branch of sociology; those describe neighbouring disciplines. Key note: some older texts call social psychology a bridge between psychology and sociology, but its focus on the individual is what separates it from sociology, so a stands.", "topic": "Scope of social psychology", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Abnormal psychology is concerned with:", "options": {"a": "Understanding", "b": "Treatment", "c": "Prevention", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: abnormal psychology is concerned with understanding, treatment and prevention of mental disorders, so all of the above apply. Abnormal psychology is the branch of psychology that studies abnormal behaviour and experience: anxiety, depression, schizophrenia, substance use and the rest. Like psychology in general, it has broad aims: to describe disorders (symptoms, course, how common they are), to explain them, to treat them and to prevent them. Understanding (option a) covers description and aetiology, the study of causes, using biological, psychological and social factors together in the biopsychosocial and diathesis-stress views. Treatment (option b) covers the therapies that grow from that understanding: medication, psychotherapy, family work and rehabilitation. Prevention (option c) is the newest aim; Gerald Caplan (1964) set out three levels. Primary prevention stops new cases (stress management in schools), secondary prevention catches cases early (screening), and tertiary prevention reduces disability in established cases (rehabilitation). Option d is right because the field covers all three. Take Arjun, a student who starts having panic attacks before exams. Abnormal psychology explains why (a learned fear of his own bodily sensations), treats it (cognitive behaviour therapy) and asks how such attacks could be prevented in other students (exam stress workshops). Exam trap: students often think abnormal psychology only describes and explains, leaving treatment to clinical psychology; in textbook definitions treatment and prevention are part of its scope too, so any single option is incomplete.", "topic": "Aims of abnormal psychology", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Phobias originate from:", "options": {"a": "Loss of touch with reality", "b": "Hallucinations", "c": "Unfounded beliefs", "d": "Unconscious motivation"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: in the classic textbook account, phobias originate from unconscious motivation. A phobia is an intense, persistent and irrational fear of a specific object or situation (heights, dogs, closed spaces) that leads to avoidance. Sigmund Freud's case of Little Hans (1909) gave the psychoanalytic explanation. Hans, a five-year-old Viennese boy, was terrified that a horse would bite him. Freud argued that his real fear was of his father, his rival in the Oedipal conflict; because this fear was unacceptable, it was repressed and displaced onto horses, which could simply be avoided. On this view a phobia is a defence: anxiety from an unconscious conflict is attached to an outside object. Option d is right because, among these choices, only unconscious motivation names a cause of phobias in a recognised theory. Phobic people keep contact with reality, usually know their fear is excessive and do not hallucinate; those features belong to psychosis. Learning theory gives the main alternative: John B. Watson and Rosalie Rayner (1920) conditioned fear in Little Albert, Joseph Wolpe and Stanley Rachman (1960) reread Little Hans as fear conditioned after he saw a horse fall, and Martin Seligman (1971) added preparedness. If Meera has feared lifts since childhood but cannot say why, a psychoanalyst would look for a hidden conflict. Exam trap: \"unfounded beliefs\" sounds right because phobic fear is irrational, but it describes the fear, not where it comes from. Key note: modern cognitive accounts do stress catastrophic beliefs, but these are seen mainly as keeping a phobia going; as an origin among these options the classic textbook answer is unconscious motivation, so d stands.", "topic": "Psychodynamic origin of phobias", "fig": "Sigmund Freud"}, {"q": "In anxiety neurosis the eliciting stimuli is not:", "options": {"a": "Specific", "b": "Obscure", "c": "Complex", "d": "Quite pervasive"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: in anxiety neurosis the eliciting stimulus is not specific. Anxiety neurosis is an older diagnostic name, introduced by Sigmund Freud (1895) when he separated it from neurasthenia; today it falls mainly under generalised anxiety disorder (GAD) and panic disorder. Its mark is free-floating anxiety: a constant state of worry and tension that is not tied to any particular object or situation. The person feels apprehensive about health, money, work and family in turn, with restlessness, muscle tension, poor sleep and poor concentration. Because the source of the anxiety cannot be pinned down, textbooks describe its eliciting stimuli as obscure (hidden, unclear), complex (many interacting worries) and pervasive (spread across most of life). Option a is right because a specific eliciting stimulus is exactly what anxiety neurosis lacks. Compare Meera and Arjun. Meera feels tense all day and cannot say why; one worry slides into the next. That is anxiety neurosis. Arjun is calm until he sees a dog, then panics and crosses the road. His fear has a specific trigger, which makes it a phobia. Exam trap: the stem is a negative (\"is not\"), so the three options that do fit anxiety neurosis (obscure, complex, pervasive) are the distractors; the odd one out, \"specific\", is the defining feature of phobic disorders.", "topic": "Free-floating anxiety", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A measure of variability is to ______ as a measure of central tendency is to ______.", "options": {"a": "Mode; median", "b": "Correlation; scatter plot", "c": "Standard deviation; mean", "d": "Histogram; frequency polygon"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: standard deviation is to variability as the mean is to central tendency. Descriptive statistics summarise a set of scores in two main ways. A measure of central tendency gives one typical value: the mean (the arithmetic average), the median (the middle score) and the mode (the most frequent score). A measure of variability (dispersion) shows how spread out the scores are around that centre: the range, the quartile deviation, the mean deviation, the variance and the standard deviation (SD). The SD is the square root of the average squared deviation from the mean; Karl Pearson (1894) introduced the term. The mean and the SD are the standard partners, reported together in almost every research paper. Option c is right because it is the only pair that gives a variability measure first and a central tendency measure second, as the stem requires. Suppose Riya's class and Kabir's class both have a mean of 60 marks, but Riya's class has an SD of 5 and Kabir's an SD of 15: the averages match, yet Kabir's class is far more spread out. Exam trap: in analogy items examiners offer pairs that belong together but do not match the stem's pattern; mode and median are both central tendency, correlation and scatter plot are both about relationships, and histogram and frequency polygon are both graphs.", "topic": "Variability and central tendency", "fig": ""}, {"q": "One student in the class got an extremely low score of 10 out of 100 on a test. Which measure of central tendency is most affected by this low score?", "options": {"a": "Mean", "b": "Median", "c": "Mode", "d": "Range"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: the mean is the measure of central tendency most affected by one extremely low score. The mean is calculated from the value of every score: add them all and divide by the number of scores. So a single extreme score (an outlier) pulls the mean towards itself. The median is the middle score when scores are put in order; it depends only on position, so an extreme score at one end hardly moves it. The mode is the most frequent score and is not touched by a single unusual value. Option a is right because only the mean uses the actual size of every score. Take five students with marks of 60, 65, 70, 75 and 80. The mean is 70 and the median is 70. Now suppose Arjun's 60 is replaced by a 10: the marks are 10, 65, 70, 75 and 80. The mean drops to 60, but the median stays at 70. This is why the median is preferred for skewed data such as family income, where a few very rich households inflate the mean. Exam trap: range is even more affected by an extreme score (here it jumps from 20 to 70), but range is a measure of variability, not of central tendency, so it does not answer the stem.", "topic": "Mean and extreme scores", "fig": ""}, {"q": "For his class presentation, Liam prepared a graph that depicted a frequency distribution with vertical bars that touched each other. Liam has constructed a:", "options": {"a": "Scatter diagram", "b": "Frequency polygon", "c": "Histogram", "d": "Standard deviation"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: a graph of a frequency distribution with vertical bars that touch each other is a histogram. To build one, scores are grouped into class intervals (say 30 to 39, 40 to 49, 50 to 59) marked along the horizontal axis, with frequency on the vertical axis. Over each interval a rectangle is drawn whose height equals the number of scores in it. The bars touch because the score scale is continuous: one interval ends exactly where the next begins. The word histogram is usually credited to Karl Pearson in the 1890s. Option c is right because touching bars over a frequency distribution are the defining look of a histogram. If Kabir plots the marks of 40 classmates this way, the bars show at a glance where most marks fall and whether the distribution is skewed. Related graphs differ in small ways. Join the midpoints of the bar tops with straight lines, close the line on the baseline, and you get a frequency polygon, useful for overlaying two distributions. Separate bars with gaps form a bar graph, used for categories such as religion or state of birth. Exam trap: students confuse the histogram with the bar graph; gaps mean separate categories, touching bars mean continuous class intervals.", "topic": "Histogram", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Range is to mode as ______ is to ______.", "options": {"a": "Correlation; scatter diagram", "b": "Median; mode", "c": "Correlation coefficient; z score", "d": "Variability; central tendency"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: range is to mode as variability is to central tendency. This analogy asks what each term is an example of. The range is the simplest measure of variability: the highest score minus the lowest score. It shows how widely scores are spread but depends only on the two extreme scores. The mode is the simplest measure of central tendency: the score that occurs most often, and the only average that can be used with nominal data such as favourite colour. So the relation is \"example of a category\": range belongs to variability as mode belongs to central tendency. Option d is right because it names the two categories in the same order as the stem. The other members of each family follow the same pattern: interquartile range, variance and standard deviation for variability; mean and median for central tendency. For Meera's test scores of 40, 55, 55, 60 and 90, the range is 50 and the mode is 55. Exam trap: option b looks tempting because mode appears in it, but median and mode are both central tendency, so the pair does not mirror the stem; options a and c pair ideas from correlation and standard scores.", "topic": "Range and mode analogy", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In a book entitled \"Choosing a Vocation\" who has described a three-part model for career counseling?", "options": {"a": "Gladding", "b": "Kottler", "c": "Parsons", "d": "Watson"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: Frank Parsons described the three-part model of career counselling in Choosing a Vocation (1909). Parsons, a Boston social reformer, opened the Vocation Bureau in Boston in 1908 to help young people find suitable work; he died later that year, and his book was published after his death. It made him the father of vocational guidance. His model, later called the trait-and-factor approach, has three steps: (1) a clear understanding of yourself, your aptitudes, abilities, interests, ambitions, resources and limitations; (2) knowledge of the requirements, conditions of success, advantages, pay and prospects of different kinds of work; and (3) \"true reasoning\" on the relations between these two groups of facts, that is, matching the person to the job. Later aptitude and interest tests were built to supply the first step scientifically. Option c is right because both the book title and the three-part model are Parsons's. A counsellor using his method with Kabir would assess his aptitudes and interests, show him what engineering, teaching and design jobs actually demand, then reason with him about the best fit. Exam trap: Samuel Gladding (Counseling: A Comprehensive Profession, 1988) and Jeffrey Kottler (On Being a Therapist, 1986) are modern counselling authors whose textbooks describe Parsons's model, so their names feel familiar in the same chapter; neither wrote Choosing a Vocation.", "topic": "Parsons's trait-factor model", "fig": "Frank Parsons"}, {"q": "An activity that is designed to work primarily with those who are experiencing developmental or adjustment problems is called:", "options": {"a": "Psychotherapy", "b": "Social work", "c": "Treatment", "d": "Counseling"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: an activity designed to work mainly with people experiencing developmental or adjustment problems is counselling. Counselling is a professional, face-to-face relationship in which a trained counsellor helps a basically healthy person understand a problem of living and reach his or her own decision. Its clients are in the normal range: a student choosing a stream, a new employee struggling to fit in, a couple adjusting to a first baby, someone grieving a loss. The work is usually brief and focused on the present, on growth, prevention and coping. Psychotherapy, by contrast, works with more serious or long-standing disorders (severe depression, personality disorders) and seeks deeper and slower personality change. The two overlap so much that Carl Rogers (1942, Counseling and Psychotherapy) used the words almost interchangeably, but counselling textbooks draw the line by the severity of the problem and the depth of the goals. Option d is right because developmental and adjustment problems are the defining territory of counselling. If Riya, a first-year hostel student, feels homesick and cannot settle into college life, a college counsellor rather than a psychotherapist is the natural first help. Exam trap: psychotherapy is the near-miss, since both are talking treatments; the clue words \"developmental\" and \"adjustment\" point to counselling, while \"disorder\" or \"personality change\" point to psychotherapy.", "topic": "Counselling versus psychotherapy", "fig": ""}, {"q": "To enable clients to pay close attention to their moment-to-moment experiences so they can recognize and integrate disowned aspects of themselves is the goal of:", "options": {"a": "Gestalt counseling", "b": "Reality counseling", "c": "Adlerian counseling", "d": "Cognitive counseling"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: helping clients attend to moment-to-moment experience so they can recognise and integrate disowned parts of themselves is the goal of Gestalt counselling. Gestalt therapy was developed by Fritz Perls with Laura Perls and set out in Gestalt Therapy (1951), written by Fritz Perls, Ralph Hefferline and Paul Goodman. It borrows the Gestalt idea of the whole: a healthy person is an integrated whole, but people split off feelings and needs they cannot accept (\"I am never angry\"). These disowned parts show up as unfinished business, tension and blocked awareness. The therapist keeps the client in the here and now, asking \"what\" and \"how\" (\"What are you feeling right now? How are you holding your hands?\") rather than \"why\". Techniques include the empty chair, in which the client speaks to an imagined person or to a part of the self; two-chair dialogue between \"top dog\" and \"underdog\"; and owning statements (\"I choose\", \"I take responsibility for\"). Option a is right because awareness and integration are exactly the stated aims of Gestalt work. If Meera insists she is not hurt by her father, a Gestalt counsellor might ask her to speak to him in the empty chair and to notice her clenched fists as she does. Exam trap: the word Gestalt also names the perception school of Max Wertheimer (1912); the therapy shares the idea of wholeness but is a separate humanistic approach.", "topic": "Gestalt therapy goals", "fig": "Fritz Perls"}, {"q": "Family counseling bears some similarity to ______ counseling in that the systematic dynamics are as important as individual behavior.", "options": {"a": "Group", "b": "Couple", "c": "Individual", "d": "Sex"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: family counselling resembles group counselling because, in both, the dynamics of the system matter as much as each person's behaviour. Family counselling grew from systems thinking from the 1950s onwards, through the work of Murray Bowen (family systems theory), Virginia Satir (Conjoint Family Therapy, 1964) and Salvador Minuchin (Families and Family Therapy, 1974). Its central idea is that a family is a system: members influence one another in circular loops, so a child's problem may express the family's patterns rather than a flaw in the child alone. Group counselling, with several clients and one or two leaders, also works through interaction. Members' roles, alliances, norms and conflicts inside the group become material for change, and Irvin Yalom (1970) described therapeutic factors such as universality and cohesiveness that arise only from group life. Option a is right because group counselling is the other format in which the counsellor treats interaction patterns among several people, not just one individual. Picture Kabir, a teenager who skips school. An individual counsellor studies Kabir; a family counsellor notices that his truancy is the only thing that makes his quarrelling parents work together, much as a group leader notices who speaks for whom. Exam trap: couple counselling also involves more than one person, but it is itself a form of family work with two people; the textbook comparison is with group counselling. The printed \"systematic\" means systemic (to do with systems).", "topic": "Family and group counselling", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Guidance as a life-long process is one of the:", "options": {"a": "Goals", "b": "Principles", "c": "Assumption", "d": "Classification"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: \"guidance is a life-long process\" is one of the principles of guidance. Guidance is the help given to a person to understand himself or herself and to make wise educational, vocational and personal choices. The classic list of its principles, the basic rules that should run through any guidance programme, comes from Lester D. Crow and Alice Crow (An Introduction to Guidance, 1951) and is repeated in most Indian guidance texts. One of these principles is that guidance should be a continuing process of service from early childhood through adulthood, not a one-time event. The others: it is for all, not only for problem cases; it respects individual differences and the dignity of each person; it is cooperative, never forced; it deals with the whole person (intellectual, emotional, social, physical); and it is the joint task of teachers, parents and counsellors. These differ from the goals of guidance (what it tries to achieve: self-understanding, adjustment, sound decisions) and from its classification into types (educational, vocational, personal and social guidance). Option b is right because life-long continuity is a rule about how guidance must be carried out, which is what a principle is. Arjun needs guidance when choosing his Class 11 stream, again when picking a college course, and again when changing jobs at 35: the help continues through life. Exam trap: goals and principles are easily mixed up; goals name the outcome, principles name the rules of practice. Key note: some texts, such as the NCERT guidance module, discuss the principles and assumptions of guidance under one heading, which is why option c tempts; the standard lists, from Crow and Crow onwards, name life-long continuity as a principle, so b stands.", "topic": "Principles of guidance", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Choosing a vocation is one of the major concerns of:", "options": {"a": "Educational guidance", "b": "Social guidance", "c": "Personal guidance", "d": "Vocational guidance"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: choosing a vocation is a major concern of vocational guidance. Vocational guidance is the process of helping a person choose an occupation, prepare for it, enter it and progress in it. It began with Frank Parsons, who opened the Vocation Bureau in Boston in 1908 and whose book Choosing a Vocation (1909) gave the field its three steps: know yourself, know the world of work, and match the two by true reasoning. Modern vocational guidance adds aptitude and interest testing, career information and theories of career choice and development, such as the RIASEC types of John Holland (1959) and the life stages of Donald Super (1953). Educational guidance, a term usually traced to the 1914 dissertation of Truman Kelley, helps pupils choose subjects, courses and institutions and handle study problems. Personal and social guidance deals with emotional problems, relationships and adjustment in family and community. Option d is right because selecting an occupation is the defining aim of vocational guidance, just as the title of Parsons's book says. When Meera, finishing her degree, is unsure whether to become a teacher or a nurse, a counsellor who assesses her interests and explains each job's demands is giving vocational guidance. Exam trap: educational and vocational guidance overlap, since choosing a course often leads to a career, so students pick educational guidance; but the choice of a job itself belongs to vocational guidance.", "topic": "Vocational guidance", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A useful tool for gathering information about family relationships and structures is known as:", "options": {"a": "Genogram", "b": "Sociometry", "c": "TAT", "d": "NEO-FFI"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: a genogram is a useful tool for gathering information about family relationships and structures. A genogram is a family tree diagram covering at least three generations. Standard symbols show males as squares and females as circles, with lines for marriages, separations, divorces and children, and marks for deaths, illnesses, addictions and the quality of relationships (close, distant, conflicted, cut off). Murray Bowen made it a core tool of his family systems therapy, using it to trace patterns passed down the generations, and Monica McGoldrick and Randy Gerson standardised it in Genograms in Family Assessment (1985). Counsellors usually draw it with the client in early sessions, and patterns such as repeated divorces or heavy drinking across generations become visible on one page. Option a is right because the genogram is designed specifically to map family structure and relationships. If Kabir, who struggles with anger, draws his genogram, he may notice that his father and grandfather were also described as short-tempered and distant from their sons. Exam trap: sociometry also draws a map of relationships, but of a group (who chooses whom in a class), not of a family across generations. Key note: the option printed \"Grogram\" is a misprint for genogram; read as genogram, a stands.", "topic": "Genogram", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which one of the following is not an ethical issue in counseling?", "options": {"a": "Confidentiality", "b": "Deception", "c": "Violation of rights", "d": "Technology use"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: deception is not one of the standard ethical issues in counselling. Counselling ethics codes, such as the American Counseling Association (ACA) Code of Ethics (2014), and counselling textbooks group the issues under familiar headings. Confidentiality: what the client says stays private, with limits such as danger to self or others (the Tarasoff ruling of 1976 set a duty to protect) and child abuse. Client rights and their violation: informed consent, the right to refuse or end counselling, and freedom from exploitation and dual relationships (for example, counselling a friend or one's own student). Technology use: online counselling, email, social media and electronic records, which the 2014 ACA code covers in a section of its own. Deception, deliberately misleading people about the purpose of a procedure, is the classic issue of research ethics. Stanley Milgram's (1963) obedience study led participants to believe they were giving real shocks, and research codes now allow deception only when it is necessary and followed by debriefing. Option b is right because it is the only option that is a research-ethics heading rather than a counselling-ethics heading. If Riya's counsellor posts details of her case on social media, that breaches confidentiality and is a technology-use problem. Exam trap: \"violation of rights\" can sound like a legal rather than an ethical term, but client rights are a core ethics topic. Key note: deceiving a client would of course be wrong, but deception as a named ethical issue belongs to research ethics, while the other three are standard counselling ethics headings, so b stands.", "topic": "Ethical issues in counselling", "fig": ""}, {"q": "An attitude scale which measures only a single trait is called:", "options": {"a": "Likert scale", "b": "Thurstone scale", "c": "Guttman scale", "d": "Bogardus scale"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: an attitude scale that measures only a single trait (a unidimensional scale) is the Guttman scale. Louis Guttman (1944) developed scalogram analysis to build a cumulative scale. Statements are arranged from mild to strong so that a person who agrees with a stronger statement should also agree with every weaker one. On a scale of acceptance of a de-addiction centre, for example, the items might read \"in my city\", \"in my locality\" and \"on my street\". If Kabir accepts it on his street, he should accept it in his locality and city too, so his total score tells exactly which items he endorsed. This only works if all items lie on one dimension; the coefficient of reproducibility, which should be 0.90 or more, tests that. Option c is right because Guttman's method is designed to test whether a scale measures one trait only. The other classic scales differ. Rensis Likert's (1932) summated ratings ask for agreement on a five-point scale and add the ratings. The equal-appearing intervals scale of Louis Thurstone and Ernest Chave (1929) uses judges to give statements scale values. The social distance scale of Emory Bogardus (1925) measures how close a relation one would accept with members of a group. Exam trap: Likert scales are often treated as unidimensional in practice, but only Guttman's procedure checks it directly. Key note: Bogardus's social distance scale is also cumulative in form, but Guttman's method is the one built to test a single dimension, so c stands.", "topic": "Guttman scalogram", "fig": "Louis Guttman"}, {"q": "The law of attraction was proposed by:", "options": {"a": "Byrne", "b": "Heider", "c": "Homans", "d": "Festinger"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: the law of attraction was proposed by Donn Byrne. Working with Don Nelson (1965), Donn Byrne showed that attraction towards a person is a positive linear function of the proportion of similar attitudes that person holds; he set out the full account in The Attraction Paradigm (1971). In the classic \"bogus stranger\" method, students first filled in an attitude questionnaire. Later they saw a questionnaire supposedly filled in by another student, but actually prepared by the experimenter to agree with a chosen proportion of their own answers, and rated how much they would like that stranger. Liking rose steadily with the proportion of agreement, not with the raw number of agreements. Byrne explained this through reinforcement: agreement is rewarding because it confirms our views, and we like people linked with reward (the reinforcement-affect model, developed with Gerald Clore in the 1970s). Option a is right because the similarity-attraction law, attraction rising in a straight line with the proportion of similar attitudes, is Byrne's. If Meera agrees with Riya on 8 of 10 topics but with Arjun on only 3 of 10, the law predicts she will like Riya more. Exam trap: other famous names in attraction research are the decoys: Fritz Heider (1958) gave balance theory, George Homans (1961) social exchange theory, and Leon Festinger (1957) cognitive dissonance, besides the Westgate proximity study. Key note: the option printed \"Humans\" is a misprint for Homans; read as Homans, a stands.", "topic": "Byrne's law of attraction", "fig": "Donn Byrne"}, {"q": "The origin of the word 'prejudice' can be traced back to the:", "options": {"a": "Chinese phrase", "b": "Latin phrase", "c": "Italian phrase", "d": "Indian phrase"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: the word 'prejudice' goes back to a Latin phrase, praejudicium, meaning a judgement made beforehand. Prae means 'before' and judicium means 'judgement', so a prejudice is literally a pre-judgement: deciding about a person before the facts are in. Gordon Allport, in The Nature of Prejudice (1954), traced how the meaning shifted. In classical Latin praejudicium was a precedent, a judgement based on earlier decisions. In English it came to mean a judgement formed before due examination of the facts, a hasty judgement. Finally it took on the emotional flavour of liking or disliking that goes with such an unsupported judgement. Allport's shortest definition was 'thinking ill of others without sufficient warrant'; his fuller one was 'an antipathy based upon a faulty and inflexible generalization'. Social psychology now separates three linked ideas: a stereotype is the belief (cognitive), prejudice is the negative attitude (affective) and discrimination is the unfair action (behavioural). Example: Meera decides that a new classmate from another state is lazy before ever working with her, and keeps that view even after the classmate proves hard working; that fixed prejudgement is prejudice in Allport's sense. Option b is right because the root word, praejudicium, is Latin. Exam trap: Italian descends from Latin, so 'Italian phrase' sounds close, but Italian only has a sister word from the same root; English took 'prejudice' through Old French from the Latin praejudicium, which is the origin every textbook cites.", "topic": "Origin of the word prejudice", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The most preferred research method in experimental psychology is:", "options": {"a": "Correlational", "b": "Descriptive", "c": "Cross-cultural", "d": "Experimentation"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: experimentation is the most preferred method in experimental psychology, because only a true experiment can show cause and effect. In an experiment the researcher deliberately changes one factor, the independent variable (IV), measures its effect on behaviour, the dependent variable (DV), and holds every other factor constant or balances it out (control of extraneous variables). Random assignment of participants to conditions spreads individual differences evenly across groups, so any difference in the DV can be traced to the IV. Wilhelm Wundt (1879) founded the first psychological laboratory at Leipzig on this logic, and the field takes its very name, experimental psychology, from the method. Example: Arjun wants to know whether background music lowers recall. He randomly assigns 40 students to study a word list with or without music, keeps the list, study time and room the same, and compares recall scores. If the music group recalls less, he can conclude that the music caused the drop. Option d is right because experimentation alone combines manipulation, control and random assignment, which together allow a causal conclusion. Exam trap: the correlational method also measures two variables and can show a strong link, so it tempts, but it cannot say which variable causes which or rule out a third variable; descriptive and cross-cultural methods describe and compare without manipulating anything.", "topic": "Experimental method", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The type of fluency which indicates production of new ideas to fit a system or logical theories is called:", "options": {"a": "Ideational fluency", "b": "Expressional fluency", "c": "Associational fluency", "d": "Word fluency"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: expressional fluency is the ability to produce new ideas that fit a system, putting words and ideas together into organised phrases, sentences or a logical theory. Joy Paul Guilford (1950, 1967) split creative thinking into divergent production abilities and found that 'fluency', the sheer number of responses a person can produce, is not one ability but several. Word fluency is producing words that meet a structural rule, such as words beginning with 's'. Ideational fluency is producing many ideas that fulfil a requirement, such as uses for a brick or titles for a story. Associational fluency is producing words from a restricted area of meaning, such as synonyms for 'good'. Expressional fluency is fitting ideas into an organised whole: writing sentences from given starting letters, or linking statements into a coherent argument. In Guilford's structure of intellect model it is the divergent production of semantic systems, which is why texts describe it as producing ideas 'to fit a system or logical theories'. Example: given the letters W, C, E and N, Kabir quickly writes 'Why can't everyone nap?' and several more sentences; that skill of arranging ideas into connected form is expressional fluency. Option b is right because only expressional fluency involves organising ideas into a system rather than simply listing them. Exam trap: ideational fluency also concerns 'ideas', so it is the near miss, but it counts the number of separate ideas, not the fitting of ideas into a structured system.", "topic": "Guilford's fluency factors", "fig": "Joy Paul Guilford"}, {"q": "Who advocated strongly that creativity should not equate with intelligence?", "options": {"a": "Benett", "b": "Thurstone", "c": "Guilford", "d": "Butcher"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: Joy Paul Guilford (1950) argued strongly that creativity must not be equated with intelligence. In his presidential address to the American Psychological Association, titled 'Creativity', he complained that psychologists had almost ignored creative ability and that intelligence tests could not find it, because they reward convergent thinking: reasoning towards the one correct answer. Creativity, he said, rests on divergent thinking (later 'divergent production' in his structure of intellect model): producing many varied, original answers to an open question, scored for fluency, flexibility, originality and elaboration. Later work backed the separation. Jacob Getzels and Philip Jackson (1962) found highly creative pupils who were not the highest in IQ, and Michael Wallach and Nathan Kogan (1965) found creativity largely independent of intelligence when children were tested in relaxed, game-like conditions. The threshold hypothesis adds that IQ and creativity go together only up to an IQ of about 120. Example: Riya has an average IQ but lists 25 unusual uses for a newspaper, while her high-IQ friend Ananya lists six ordinary ones; an IQ test alone would miss Riya's talent. Option c is right because Guilford is the theorist who drew the convergent versus divergent distinction and insisted that intelligence tests do not measure creativity. Exam trap: Louis Thurstone (1938) is also a famous name in intelligence theory, but his primary mental abilities, such as verbal comprehension and number, were a multiple-factor account of intelligence, not a case for separating creativity from it.", "topic": "Creativity versus intelligence", "fig": "Joy Paul Guilford"}, {"q": "For determining subject's span of attention for digits, letters or figures, the experimenter requires subject to describe what he:", "options": {"a": "Is aware of", "b": "Knows", "c": "Reports", "d": "Observes"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: in a span of attention experiment the subject describes what he is aware of in one brief glance. The span of attention (span of apprehension) is the number of separate items, such as digits, letters, dots or figures, that a person can grasp in a single momentary act of attention. William Stanley Jevons (1871) threw handfuls of beans into a box and, glancing for an instant, estimated their number: he was never wrong with three or four, but his errors grew beyond that. In the laboratory a tachistoscope flashes a card for a fraction of a second, too short for eye movements, and the subject then states everything he was aware of; the span is the largest number of items grasped correctly. Adults typically take in about six to eight unrelated items, more if the items can be grouped. Example: Kabir sees the letters K, R, T, M, B, S and L flashed for a split second and says he was aware of K, R, T, M and B; his span for letters on that trial is five. Option a is right because the span is defined by awareness: how many items enter consciousness at once, which is exactly what the subject is asked to describe. Exam trap: 'reports' sounds right because the subject does give a verbal report, but the report is only the method; what he reports is what he was aware of. Key note: in practice the subject 'reports' what he 'observed', so c and d are defensible readings of the procedure; but the span is defined by how many items he is aware of at once, and reporting and observing are the method rather than the thing measured, so a stands.", "topic": "Span of attention", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The type of attention where attention is all the time given to the same stimulus which appears and then disappears is called:", "options": {"a": "Fluctuation of attention", "b": "Shift of attention", "c": "Span of attention", "d": "Division of attention"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: fluctuation of attention is when attention stays on the same unchanging stimulus, yet the stimulus seems to appear and disappear by turns. Attention is not steady even when we try to hold it on one object: its clearness waxes and wanes in short cycles of a few seconds. Classic demonstrations use a faint stimulus close to threshold. A watch ticking at the edge of hearing seems to fade out and come back. On the Masson disc, a white disc with a broken line of small black marks, the faintest grey ring seen when it spins vanishes and returns. Ambiguous figures such as the Necker cube flip between two views. The stimulus itself never changes, so the coming and going lies in the observer, explained by adaptation of the sense organs and by the rhythmic nature of attention itself. Example: lying in bed at night, Meera listens to the faint ticking of a clock in the next room; for some seconds she hears it clearly, then it seems to stop, then it returns, though the clock never changed. Option a is right because only fluctuation describes attention held on one stimulus that alternately appears and disappears. Exam trap: shift of attention also involves attention moving, but in a shift attention moves to a different stimulus, whereas in fluctuation the stimulus stays the same and only its clearness rises and falls.", "topic": "Fluctuation of attention", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In naturalistic observation, the experimenter observes the relationship between the independent and the ______ variables.", "options": {"a": "Stimulus", "b": "Extraneous", "c": "Dependent", "d": "Organismic"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: in naturalistic observation the researcher looks at how an independent variable relates to the dependent variable, just as in an experiment, but without manipulating anything. Naturalistic observation means watching and recording behaviour in its usual setting, such as children's play in a school yard or shoppers in a market, without intervening. The researcher cannot assign levels of a variable; instead he notes factors that already differ naturally (age, crowding, time of day) and records the behaviour of interest. The behaviour measured is the dependent variable, and the naturally varying factor plays the part of the independent variable. Because nothing is controlled, a link found this way shows a relationship, not a cause. Jane Goodall's studies of wild chimpanzees at Gombe, begun in 1960, show the method's strength: real behaviour in real settings, with high ecological validity. Example: Riya watches a school playground on crowded and quiet days and counts acts of aggression; crowding is the naturally varying independent variable and the number of aggressive acts is the dependent variable. Option c is right because the variable recorded as the outcome of the other is always the dependent variable, whatever the method. Exam trap: extraneous variables are the uncontrolled factors that worry researchers, and in naturalistic observation they are everywhere, so 'extraneous' tempts; but they are what spoils the relationship, not the second term of it.", "topic": "Variables in naturalistic observation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In the disjunctive reaction time, there are two or more different stimuli presented in a:", "options": {"a": "Non-random order", "b": "Random order", "c": "Descending order", "d": "Ascending order"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: in disjunctive reaction time, two or more different stimuli are presented in a random order. Reaction time (RT) is the interval between the onset of a stimulus and the start of the response. Franciscus Cornelis Donders (1868) compared three kinds. In simple RT (his 'a' reaction) there is one stimulus and one response, so the subject knows exactly what is coming. In the discrimination or 'c' reaction, several stimuli are shown and the subject responds to only one of them. In the choice or 'b' reaction, several stimuli each have their own response. The last two are disjunctive (compound) reactions, because the subject must first tell which stimulus has appeared. Random order is essential: if the stimuli came in a fixed sequence, the subject would learn it and anticipate, and the task would collapse into simple RT. By subtraction Donders estimated the time taken by mental steps (c minus a gives discrimination time; b minus c gives choice time), founding mental chronometry. Disjunctive RTs are always longer than simple RTs. Example: Arjun must press a key only when a red light appears, not for green or yellow, and the lights come in random order; his RT is longer than when only the red light is ever shown. Option b is right because only a random order prevents anticipation and forces the subject to discriminate. Exam trap: ascending and descending order belong to the method of limits of Gustav Theodor Fechner (1860) in psychophysics, not to reaction time experiments.", "topic": "Disjunctive reaction time", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The method of minimal changes is also known as the:", "options": {"a": "Method of average error", "b": "Method of limits", "c": "Method of constant stimuli", "d": "Differential threshold"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: the method of minimal changes is another name for the method of limits. Gustav Theodor Fechner, in Elements of Psychophysics (1860), set out three classical methods for measuring thresholds. In the method of limits the experimenter changes the stimulus in small steps (minimal changes) in ascending and descending series: a faint tone is made louder step by step until the subject first says 'yes, I hear it', and a clear tone is made softer until he says 'no'. The threshold is the average of these change points. Two constant errors are balanced by alternating the series: the error of habituation (giving the same answer too long) and the error of expectation or anticipation (changing the answer too early). Because the subject's limits are explored in a series of small steps, it is also called the method of serial exploration. The method of constant stimuli presents a fixed set of values many times in random order (the method of right and wrong cases); the method of average error lets the subject adjust the stimulus himself until it matches a standard (the method of adjustment or reproduction). Example: to find Meera's touch threshold, the tester raises the pressure on her skin in tiny steps until she first feels it, then lowers it from above until she no longer does; averaging the two points gives her threshold. Option b is right because 'minimal changes' describes exactly the small serial steps of the method of limits. Exam trap: the differential threshold (just noticeable difference) is a quantity these methods measure, not a method itself.", "topic": "Psychophysical methods", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A characteristic or attribute that can take on a number of values is called a:", "options": {"a": "Virtue", "b": "Relations", "c": "Variable", "d": "Experiment"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: a characteristic or attribute that can take on a number of values is called a variable. A variable is anything that varies: it differs from person to person, or within the same person across time or conditions. Height, age, anxiety, intelligence, reaction time, gender and social class are all variables, because each can take at least two values. Its opposite is a constant, which has only one value in a given study: if all participants are 20-year-old women, age and sex are constants there. Variables are classified in several ways. By role, the independent variable is the one manipulated or used to group people, and the dependent variable is the measured outcome. By nature, continuous variables can take any value in a range (weight, time), while discrete variables take only separate values (number of siblings). Categorical variables sort people into kinds (religion), and quantitative variables differ in amount (income). Example: Kabir records how many hours each classmate slept last night; because the answer is 5 for one, 7 for another and 8 for a third, sleep duration is a variable. Option c is right because a variable is defined precisely as an attribute that can take different values. Exam trap: 'experiment' is the method that manipulates variables, and 'relations' are what research looks for between variables; both appear in the same chapter, but neither is the attribute that varies.", "topic": "Definition of a variable", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Verbal communication is organized by:", "options": {"a": "Less cognitive content", "b": "Language systems", "c": "Expression of emotional states", "d": "None of the above"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: verbal communication is organised by language systems. Verbal communication is the sending of messages in words, spoken or written, and words carry meaning only because they belong to a language: a shared, rule-governed system of arbitrary symbols. Every language is organised at several levels: phonemes (the basic sounds), morphemes (the smallest units of meaning, such as 'un-' in 'unhappy'), syntax (rules for combining words into sentences), semantics (meaning) and pragmatics (how language is used in context). Charles Francis Hockett (1960) listed the design features these rules make possible, such as productivity (a small set of sounds yields unlimited new messages) and displacement (talking about things far away in time and place). Verbal communication therefore carries rich cognitive content: facts, ideas, arguments and plans. Non-verbal communication (facial expression, gaze, gesture, posture, tone of voice, touch) works differently: it carries less cognitive content and is the main channel for emotions and attitudes towards others. Example: Ananya tells her friend the exact time and place of a meeting (verbal, language based), while her smile and warm tone tell him she is glad he is coming (non-verbal). Option b is right because the organisation of verbal communication comes from the rules of the language system. Exam trap: options a and c are true descriptions, but of non-verbal communication, so they catch anyone who knows the verbal versus non-verbal contrast but reverses it under time pressure.", "topic": "Verbal communication and language", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Non verbal communication in man is used for managing the immediate:", "options": {"a": "Social situation", "b": "Supporting the verbal communication", "c": "Replacing the verbal communication", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "d", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is d: non-verbal communication in humans serves all three uses listed, managing the immediate social situation, supporting verbal communication and replacing verbal communication. The British social psychologist Michael Argyle (1972; Bodily Communication, 1975) described these as the main uses of non-verbal communication (NVC) in human interaction. First, NVC manages the immediate social situation: through facial expression, gaze, posture, distance and touch, people signal emotions and interpersonal attitudes such as liking, dominance or hostility, and so set up and regulate the relationship of the moment. Second, it sustains or supports verbal communication: nods, glances and pauses control turn-taking, give the speaker feedback and add emphasis. Third, it replaces verbal communication when speech is impossible or unwanted, as with gestures across a noisy room or the sign languages of deaf people. He also noted its part in self-presentation and in rituals such as greetings. Example: when Kabir joins a group at a party, their smiles and open posture tell him he is welcome (managing the situation), his friend's nods keep him talking (supporting speech), and a wave from across the room says 'over here' without a word (replacing speech). Option d is right because options a, b and c are, word for word, Argyle's three uses of NVC, so all of them apply. Exam trap: the classic first use, managing the social situation, is often taught alone, so a single option tempts. Key note: as printed, the stem ends 'used for managing the immediate', which only 'social situation' completes, so a can be defended on the literal wording. But the options copy Argyle's three-part list plus 'All of the above', which shows the phrase slipped into the stem from option a; the intended question is what NVC is used for, so d stands.", "topic": "Uses of non-verbal communication", "fig": "Michael Argyle"}, {"q": "India's population is growing faster than that of:", "options": {"a": "Korea", "b": "Indonesia", "c": "China", "d": "Pakistan"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: India's population is growing faster than China's. The population growth rate is the yearly percentage increase in a population: births minus deaths (natural increase) plus net migration. China slowed its growth sharply through its one-child policy, in force from 1979 to 2015, so its rate fell to about half a per cent a year by the 2010s, and its population began to shrink in 2022. India never had such a rule, and its rate stayed well above China's, at about 1.2 to 1.3 per cent a year around 2014; the United Nations estimated that India overtook China as the world's most populous country in April 2023. Pakistan, by contrast, has grown faster than India for decades (about 2 per cent a year or more), so India is not growing faster than Pakistan. Environmental and social psychology texts use these figures in chapters on population density and crowding, where rapid growth puts pressure on housing, transport and resources. Example: Arjun's textbook table shows China at about 0.5 per cent growth and India at about 1.2 per cent, so India adds people faster even though China was then the larger country. Option c is right because India's growth rate has been well above China's ever since China's birth-control policies took hold. Exam trap: Pakistan is a neighbour with a similar culture, so it seems a natural comparison, but its growth rate is higher than India's, not lower. Key note: India also grows faster than both North and South Korea, whose rates are well under 1 per cent a year, and on some estimates faster than Indonesia, whose rate has been close to India's; so a, and arguably b, are also literally true. The textbook comparison is between the two population giants, India and China, so c stands.", "topic": "Population growth comparison", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Air pollution has negative effects on our interpersonal interactions and:", "options": {"a": "Health", "b": "Mind", "c": "Heart", "d": "Emotions"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: air pollution has negative effects on our interpersonal interactions and health. Environmental psychology studies how physical surroundings affect behaviour, and air pollution (smog, vehicle exhaust, industrial smoke, carbon monoxide, foul odours and second-hand cigarette smoke) is one of its classic stressors. Its health effects are well known: irritation of the eyes and throat, asthma and other respiratory disease, heart disease, and headaches and dizziness from carbon monoxide. Its social effects are less obvious. Laboratory studies by James Rotton and colleagues (1979) found that moderately foul-smelling air made participants more aggressive towards another person, and unpleasant air in general lowers mood, which in turn makes people more irritable and less pleasant company. Polluted days also keep people indoors and away from others. Example: on a heavy smog day in Delhi, Riya's cough worsens (health) and she snaps at her younger brother over nothing (interpersonal interaction). Option a is right because health is the broad second area, alongside social behaviour, that research on air pollution consistently shows is harmed. Exam trap: heart, mind and emotions are each affected too, so each feels plausible; but they are narrower parts of health or the emotional route to poorer interaction, and the textbook pairing is 'interpersonal interactions and health'.", "topic": "Effects of air pollution", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Personality determines how people react to:", "options": {"a": "Water", "b": "Noise", "c": "Pollution", "d": "Health risks"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: personality determines how people react to noise. Noise is unwanted sound, and environmental psychology shows that its effects depend not only on loudness but on the listener. In a study of a college dormitory, Neil Weinstein (1978) measured students' noise sensitivity, a stable personality trait, when they arrived; over the year the noise-sensitive students were more disturbed by dormitory noise, and less able to get used to it, than the less sensitive ones. Other individual differences matter too: introverts, who are more easily aroused, tend to be more bothered by noise than extraverts, and anxious people tolerate it less. Control and predictability also shape reactions. David Glass and Jerome Singer (Urban Stress, 1972) found that people exposed to loud, unpredictable bursts of noise later showed less persistence on puzzles, but these after-effects shrank when people believed they could stop the noise by pressing a button, even if they never pressed it. Example: in the same hostel corridor, Kabir sleeps through loud music while his roommate Arjun, who is highly noise sensitive, lies awake irritated; the noise is the same, their personalities differ. Option b is right because personality traits such as noise sensitivity and introversion are classic, well-studied determinants of reactions to noise. Exam trap: pollution and health risks also draw different reactions from different people, so they seem plausible, but the textbook sentence comes from the noise section. Key note: personality also shapes reactions to pollution and health risks, so the item is loose; it comes from the noise section of the environmental psychology chapter, where personality determines how people react to noise, so b stands.", "topic": "Personality and noise", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Important properties that make scales of measurement different from one another are:", "options": {"a": "Magnitude and an absolute zero", "b": "Magnitude and equal intervals", "c": "Equal intervals and an absolute zero", "d": "Magnitude, equal intervals, and an absolute zero"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: the properties that make scales of measurement different from one another are magnitude, equal intervals and an absolute zero. Stanley Smith Stevens (1946) sorted measurement into four levels, and each level is defined by which of these three properties its numbers have. Magnitude means the numbers show more or less of the attribute (order). Equal intervals means one unit stands for the same amount anywhere on the scale. An absolute (true) zero means zero stands for a complete absence of the attribute. A nominal scale has none of the three: numbers are just labels, like a cricketer's shirt number. An ordinal scale has magnitude only: class ranks show order but not how far apart students are. An interval scale has magnitude and equal intervals but an arbitrary zero: 0 degrees Celsius is not an absence of heat, and IQ scores work the same way, so ratios are meaningless. A ratio scale has all three: weight, reaction time and income, where 40 kg really is twice 20 kg. Example: Meera's class rank (ordinal), the temperature of her classroom in Celsius (interval) and her reaction time in milliseconds (ratio) each tell her teacher progressively more, because each level adds one property. Option d is right because all three properties are needed to separate the four levels. Exam trap: options a, b and c each name a correct pair, so they feel right, but each drops one property: without absolute zero you cannot tell interval from ratio, without equal intervals you cannot tell ordinal from interval, and without magnitude you cannot tell nominal from ordinal.", "topic": "Properties of measurement scales", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The term stanine comes from:", "options": {"a": "Decile", "b": "Standard score", "c": "Standard nine", "d": "Range"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: the term stanine comes from 'standard nine'. A stanine is a normalised standard score on a nine-point scale running from 1 to 9, with a mean of 5 and a standard deviation (SD) of about 2. It was developed by the United States Army Air Forces during World War II, partly so that each score would fit in a single column of a punch card. Stanines are assigned by rank: the lowest 4 per cent of the norm group get stanine 1, the next 7 per cent get 2, and so on in the proportions 4, 7, 12, 17, 20, 17, 12, 7 and 4 per cent. Every band except 1 and 9 is half an SD wide; stanine 5 covers the middle 20 per cent, from z = minus 0.25 to plus 0.25. Because each stanine is a broad band, small chance differences in raw score rarely change it, which discourages over-reading tiny gaps between pupils. Related scales are the sten ('standard ten', mean 5.5, SD 2) and the C scale with 11 units. Example: Riya's raw score on an aptitude test falls at the 50th percentile, so she gets stanine 5; Kabir's falls at the 97th percentile, so he gets stanine 9. Option c is right because 'stanine' is a contraction of STAndard NINE. Exam trap: the stanine is a standard score, so option b truly describes what it is, but the question asks where the word comes from, and the word is built from 'standard nine'.", "topic": "Stanine scores", "fig": ""}, {"q": "\"Getting ready to read\" is the developmental task of:", "options": {"a": "Babyhood and early childhood", "b": "Late childhood", "c": "Early childhood", "d": "Old age"}, "correct": "a", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is a: 'getting ready to read' is a developmental task of babyhood and early childhood. A developmental task, in the idea of Robert J. Havighurst (Developmental Tasks and Education, 1948), is a task that arises at or about a certain period of life; mastering it brings happiness and success with later tasks, while failure brings unhappiness, social disapproval and later difficulty. Tasks come from physical maturation (walking), cultural pressure (reading) and personal values (choosing a career). Elizabeth Hurlock (1898 to 1988), in Developmental Psychology: A Life-Span Approach, lists Havighurst's tasks for babyhood and early childhood together, roughly birth to six years: learning to walk, to take solid food, to talk and to control elimination; learning sex differences and modesty; getting ready to read; and learning to tell right from wrong and beginning a conscience. Late childhood (about 6 to 12) brings the next set: physical skills for ordinary games, getting along with age-mates, and the fundamental skills of reading, writing and calculating. So getting ready to read belongs to the earlier period, and actually learning to read to the later one. Example: four-year-old Meera listens to stories, turns pages and recognises the letters of her name; she is getting ready to read, long before formal reading lessons in primary school. Option a is right because the task is listed under the combined heading babyhood and early childhood. Exam trap: late childhood is a near miss, because reading itself is learned then, but the stem says getting ready to read. Key note: getting ready to read happens in the early childhood years, so c looks right, but the task list is published under the combined heading 'babyhood and early childhood', so a stands.", "topic": "Developmental tasks of early childhood", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Thirteen to eighteen years is the period of:", "options": {"a": "Early adulthood", "b": "Adolescence", "c": "Early childhood", "d": "Preadolescence"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: thirteen to eighteen years is the period of adolescence. Elizabeth Hurlock (1898 to 1988), whose Developmental Psychology: A Life-Span Approach is the standard text in Indian courses, divides life into ten periods: prenatal (conception to birth), infancy (birth to 2 weeks), babyhood (2 weeks to 2 years), early childhood (2 to 6 years), late childhood (6 to 10 or 12 years), puberty or preadolescence (10 or 12 to 13 or 14 years), adolescence (13 or 14 to 18 years), early adulthood (18 to 40), middle age (40 to 60) and old age (60 onward). She further splits adolescence into early adolescence (13 to 16 or 17) and late adolescence (16 or 17 to 18, the age of legal maturity). Adolescence is the transition from childhood to adulthood, marked by the search for identity, growing independence from parents and the rising importance of peers; Granville Stanley Hall (1904) called it a period of 'storm and stress'. Example: Riya at 15 is in early adolescence and preoccupied with how her friends see her; her 11-year-old cousin, just entering the growth spurt, is in puberty; her 25-year-old brother is in early adulthood. Option b is right because Hurlock places adolescence at 13 or 14 to 18 years, which matches the stem. Exam trap: preadolescence (puberty) overlaps the start of the teens, so it tempts, but in Hurlock's scheme it is the short bridging period of about 10 or 12 to 13 or 14 years, which ends as adolescence begins.", "topic": "Hurlock's life span periods", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which one of the following factors does not contribute to interpretation of symptoms?", "options": {"a": "Prior experience", "b": "Expectations", "c": "Seriousness of the symptoms", "d": "Health beliefs"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: health beliefs are not among the factors that shape how a person interprets a symptom. Shelley E. Taylor, in her textbook Health Psychology (1986), describes how people first notice bodily symptoms and then interpret them before deciding whether to seek care. Interpretation depends on three things. Prior experience: a person who has had a symptom before judges it by what happened last time, so familiar symptoms are often taken less seriously. Expectations: people notice and interpret symptoms they expect, as when someone told that flu is going round reads a sore throat as flu. Seriousness of the symptom: symptoms that cause pain, disrupt daily life or threaten a valued body part such as the eyes or heart are judged serious and acted on quickly. The interpretation then feeds a mental picture of the illness (its identity, cause, consequences, duration and cure), as in the common-sense model of Howard Leventhal (1980). Health beliefs belong to a different idea, the Health Belief Model of Irwin Rosenstock (1966): perceived susceptibility, severity, benefits and barriers, which predict whether a person takes up preventive health behaviour. Example: Arjun had acidity last year, so he dismisses new chest pain as 'gas' (prior experience), though its crushing quality should tell him it is serious. Option d is right because Taylor's factors in interpretation are prior experience, expectations and seriousness, while health beliefs explain preventive behaviour. Exam trap: health beliefs sound as if they should colour interpretation, so d looks like a real factor. Key note: health beliefs do influence what a person does about symptoms, which is why d is tempting to rule out; but Taylor lists prior experience, expectations and seriousness as the factors in interpretation, so d stands.", "topic": "Interpretation of symptoms", "fig": "Shelley E. Taylor"}]};
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"jammu-2014": [{"q": "According to Gestalt theory, which one is right?", "concept": "The answer is b: Gestalt psychologists held that perceptual organisation is natural, that is, inborn and built into the way the brain works. Gestalt psychology began with Max Wertheimer's (1912) paper on the phi phenomenon, the movement we see when two lights flash one after the other, and was developed by Wolfgang Köhler (1929) and Kurt Koffka (1935). Their slogan was that the whole is different from the sum of its parts. The brain does not wait for experience to teach it how to group sensations; it organises them into wholes at once, by built-in laws: figure and ground, proximity, similarity, closure, good continuation and the master law of Prägnanz (we see the simplest, most complete form the conditions allow). Example: when Riya sees a circle drawn with a few gaps, she sees a whole circle immediately; nobody had to train her in closure. The opposite camp were the empiricists, such as Hermann von Helmholtz (1867), who said we learn to perceive through experience and unconscious inference. Evidence often cited for the inborn side, though not Gestalt work itself, is the visual cliff of Eleanor Gibson and Richard Walk (1960): crawling infants and newborn animals avoid an apparent drop. Option b is right because the Gestalt school treated organisation as a native property of the perceiving brain, not something acquired by learning. Exam trap: option d, partly natural and partly acquired, is the balanced modern view and sounds wiser, but the stem asks what Gestalt theory said, not what today's textbooks conclude.", "traps": {"a": "No school called perceptual organisation a false criterion; the Gestalt psychologists made organisation the very centre of their theory, so this option contradicts them.", "c": "Acquired organisation is the empiricist view of Hermann von Helmholtz and the British associationists, who said we learn to group sensations through experience; Gestalt theory argued the opposite.", "d": "Partly natural and partly acquired is the modern interactionist position drawn from later developmental research; it is a fair conclusion today but it is not what Gestalt theory claimed."}}, {"q": "Perceptions are not affected by:", "concept": "The answer is d: behaviour is not one of the factors that shape perception; it is the outcome that perception guides. Perception is selective: out of all the sensations reaching us, personal (internal) factors decide which we notice and how we interpret them. Textbooks list these determinants: needs and motives, emotional states, set (expectation) and suggestion, past experience and values. Needs: David McClelland and John Atkinson (1948) kept navy men without food for 1, 4 or 16 hours and asked them to identify faint pictures on a screen that was actually blank; food-related answers rose with the hours of hunger. Values: Jerome Bruner and Cecile Goodman (1947) found that poor children overestimated the size of coins more than rich children did. Emotion: tension and anxiety make us quicker to spot threats (perceptual vigilance) or slower to recognise upsetting words (perceptual defence). Suggestion and set: what we are told to expect prepares us to see it. Example: told that a new classmate is a thief, Kabir reads the boy's ordinary glance at his bag as shifty. Option d is right because behaviour comes after perception: we act on what we perceive, so behaviour is the product of the process, not one of the listed influences on it. Exam trap: hunger, anxiety and suggestion sound like things outside perception, but they are exactly the textbook determinants. Key note: behaviour and perception do interact, since what we do (moving the eyes, turning the head) changes what we attend to. But in the textbook list of perceptual determinants (needs, emotions, set and suggestion) behaviour is the odd one out, so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Hunger is a need, a classic internal determinant: David McClelland and John Atkinson's hungry navy men 'saw' more food in a blank screen as deprivation grew, so hunger does affect perception.", "b": "Tension and anxiety are emotional factors that produce perceptual vigilance or perceptual defence, so they clearly affect what is perceived.", "c": "Suggestion works through perceptual set: telling a person what to expect primes him to see it, a standard determinant of perception."}}, {"q": "When Mohan was one year old, he did not walk properly; reaching to two he moves perfectly. What was the reason?", "concept": "The answer is b: Mohan walked well at two because of maturation, the natural unfolding of his body and nervous system. Maturation means changes that come from inherited, biologically timed growth of the brain, nerves, bones and muscles. They appear on a genetic timetable and need little or no practice; maturation supplies readiness, and learning builds on it. Arnold Gesell (1929) made the idea famous. With Helen Thompson he used co-twin control: identical twin girls, T and C, so heredity was held constant. Twin T practised stair climbing every day for six weeks from 46 weeks of age. Twin C began only at 53 weeks, yet after two weeks of practice she had caught up. Training given before the body was ready added little. Walking is the textbook example. Most babies walk alone at about 12 to 15 months, but the timing varies. At one year Mohan's leg muscles, balance and brain control were not yet ready; a year later they were, and he walked smoothly without anyone teaching him. Option b is right because the improvement came with age and readiness, not from any training or experience mentioned in the stem. Exam trap: growth and maturation are often confused. Growth is quantitative, an increase in size (height, weight); maturation is the ripening of structures that makes a new function possible. Learning and practice both depend on experience, which is the opposite of maturation.", "traps": {"a": "Growth is an increase in size or quantity (height, weight, number of cells); Mohan surely grew, but bigger size alone does not explain a new skill, which comes from maturing nerves and muscles.", "c": "Learning is change through experience and practice (reading, cycling); walking is not taught, and the stem mentions no training.", "d": "Practice polishes a skill once a child is ready, but Arnold Gesell's co-twin study showed that practice before readiness adds little; nothing in the stem says Mohan was drilled."}}, {"q": "Which of the following paradigms represents classical conditioning?", "concept": "The answer is c: the shorthand US-UR/CS-CR represents classical conditioning, with the unconditioned stimulus tied to its unconditioned response and the conditioned stimulus tied to its conditioned response. Ivan Pavlov, whose lectures appeared in English as Conditioned Reflexes (1927), found classical conditioning while studying digestion in dogs. Before conditioning, food in the mouth (the unconditioned stimulus, US) produces salivation automatically (the unconditioned response, UR): an unlearned reflex. During conditioning, a neutral stimulus such as a metronome or bell is presented just before the food, many times. After conditioning, the sound alone (now the conditioned stimulus, CS) produces salivation (now the conditioned response, CR). So the paradigm has two links: US to UR, which the animal is born with, and CS to CR, which it learns. The slash in option c separates them: the inborn reflex on one side, the learned connection on the other. Example: Arjun's mother always calls 'Lunch!' just before serving his favourite rajma. Rajma (US) makes him salivate (UR); after some weeks, hearing 'Lunch!' alone (CS) makes his mouth water (CR). Option c is right because it is the only option that keeps each stimulus with its own response. Exam trap: options that string the letters in one line look like a time sequence, but they mix up which response belongs to which stimulus; the US never produces the CR and the CS never produces the UR. Key note: some books draw the paradigm as a three-stage diagram (before, during, after) rather than this shorthand, but only c keeps US with UR and CS with CR, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "US-CS-UR lists the two stimuli and then only the unlearned response; it leaves out the conditioned response, the whole product of conditioning.", "b": "US-CS-CR drops the unconditioned response and implies the US leads to the CR; in Ivan Pavlov's scheme the food (US) produces the UR, and only the bell (CS) comes to produce the CR.", "d": "US-UR-CS-CR has the right four terms but chains them as one sequence, as if the UR led to the CS; it loses the two separate pairings, inborn US-UR and learned CS-CR, that the slash in c shows."}}, {"q": "Previous learning that interferes with the recall of present learning is known as:", "concept": "The answer is c: when earlier (previous) learning interferes with the recall of later (present) learning, it is called proactive inhibition, also called proactive interference. Interference theory explains much forgetting: memories compete, and similar material competes most. 'Pro' means forward, so in proactive inhibition old learning acts forward in time and spoils the new. 'Retro' means backward, so in retroactive inhibition new learning acts backward and spoils the old. The laboratory designs show the difference. Proactive: the experimental group learns list A, then list B, and is tested on B; the control group learns only B. Retroactive: the experimental group learns A, then B, and is tested on A; the control group rests instead of learning B. Georg Müller and Alfons Pilzecker (1900) first described retroactive inhibition. Benton Underwood (1957) then reviewed many studies and showed that the more lists people had learned before, the more of a new list they forgot after a day, so proactive interference explains much everyday forgetting. Example: Kabir gets a new mobile number, but when a form asks for it he keeps writing his old one. The old number (previous learning) is blocking the new one: proactive inhibition. Option c is right because the stem describes interference running forward, from previous learning to present learning. Exam trap: the two terms are often reversed. Remember: proactive = old blocks new; retroactive = new blocks old.", "traps": {"a": "Regressive inhibition is not a standard memory term; it may be mixed up with regression, Sigmund Freud's defence mechanism of returning to an earlier stage, which has nothing to do with interference.", "b": "Retroactive inhibition is the reverse: new learning interferes with recall of old learning, as Georg Müller and Alfons Pilzecker (1900) showed.", "d": "Progressive inhibition is not a memory term; the forward-acting interference described in the stem is called proactive inhibition."}}, {"q": "Motivation could be described as:", "concept": "The answer is b: motivation is best described as an energiser of behaviour, the inner process that arouses, directs and sustains action towards a goal. The word comes from the Latin movere, 'to move'. The usual cycle runs: a need (a lack, such as food) produces a drive (an aroused state, hunger), which energises goal-directed behaviour until the goal is reached and the drive is reduced. Clark Hull (1943) built the energising idea into his drive-reduction theory: reaction potential = drive x habit strength. Habit decides which response is made; drive supplies the energy. If drive is zero, even a strong habit is not performed. Motivation also gives direction (it picks the goal) and persistence (it keeps behaviour going despite obstacles), but energising is the feature textbooks place first: 'motivation energises and directs behaviour'. Example: Riya has known how to solve past papers all year (habit), but only in the month before the entrance does motivation energise her to revise eight hours a day and keep at it. Option b is right because it names the core function of motivation: it arouses and powers behaviour rather than merely changing it. Exam trap: options c and d look scholarly because they mention needs, but they describe kinds of motives (inborn or learned), not what motivation is.", "traps": {"a": "A modifier of behaviour is too broad: learning, drugs, fatigue and punishment also modify behaviour; motivation's defining job is to arouse and direct it.", "c": "An instinctual response to a need describes William McDougall's (1908) instinct theory, one historical account of where motives come from; many human motives are not instinctive, so it is not a description of motivation itself.", "d": "An acquired response to a need describes learned (secondary) motives such as achievement or money; motivation also includes unlearned biological drives, so this is one type, not the general description."}}, {"q": "According to James-Lange theory of emotions, felt emotions depend on the:", "concept": "The answer is c: according to the James-Lange theory, the felt emotion depends on our perception of bodily changes. William James (1884), in his paper 'What is an emotion?', and the Danish physician Carl Lange (1885) reached the same idea independently. Common sense says: we see a bear, feel afraid, then tremble and run. James reversed the order: the stimulus triggers bodily changes first (racing heart, trembling, tears), and our perception of those changes is the emotion. In his words, we feel sorry because we cry, angry because we strike, afraid because we tremble. Take away the bodily changes and, for James, nothing is left but a cold judgement. Example: Arjun sees a snake on the path at night; his heart pounds and his legs shake, and sensing that pounding and shaking is his fear. Walter Cannon (1927) attacked the theory: the viscera react too slowly, the same arousal occurs in different emotions, and animals whose visceral feedback was cut still showed emotional behaviour. Later work on facial feedback revived a milder form of the idea. Option c is right because James and Lange made emotion the awareness of the body's response, with no extra step of interpretation. Exam trap: option a, interpretation of bodily arousal, sounds almost the same, but it is the two-factor theory of Stanley Schachter and Jerome Singer (1962), which adds a cognitive label to arousal.", "traps": {"a": "Interpreting (labelling) bodily arousal is Stanley Schachter and Jerome Singer's (1962) two-factor theory: arousal plus a label taken from the situation; James-Lange needs no label, only the perception of the changes.", "b": "Higher brain processes, the cognitive appraisal of a situation, are central to appraisal theories (Magda Arnold, 1960; Richard Lazarus, 1966); for James and Lange the feeling follows from the body, not from appraisal.", "d": "Lower brain areas point to Walter Cannon and Philip Bard's theory, in which the thalamus sends signals to the cortex (felt emotion) and the body (arousal) at the same time; it was proposed as a critique of James-Lange."}}, {"q": "The group factor theory of intelligence was proposed by:", "concept": "The answer is a: among the names offered, the group factor theory of intelligence was proposed by Truman L. Kelley (1928). Group factor theory says intelligence is made of several group factors, abilities shared by a cluster of tests (all the verbal tests, all the number tests), lying between one general factor and many specific ones. It answered Charles Spearman (1904), whose two-factor theory allowed only a general factor g plus a specific factor s for each test; later analysts found clusters of tests that correlated with each other more than g alone could explain. Kelley, in Crossroads in the Mind of Man, analysed test scores of school children, argued against a single dominant g and identified group factors such as verbal, number and spatial ability, memory and speed. Louis Thurstone (1938) developed the approach most fully with his seven primary mental abilities, and many textbooks name Kelley alongside Thurstone as a founder of group factor theory. Example: Meera scores high on vocabulary and reading tests but only average on arithmetic and spatial puzzles; a group factor theorist says her verbal tests share a verbal factor that her number tests do not. Option a is right because Kelley is a named founder of group factors and the only one listed here. Exam trap: Thurstone is the usual answer; when he is missing, students reach for Spearman because his theory is also a factor theory, but Spearman's factors are general and specific, not group. Key note: the same stem on the PGIBAMS Raipur 2023 paper offers Thurstone and is answered with Thurstone. This paper leaves Thurstone out, so Kelley, the other named founder of group factor theory, stands.", "traps": {"b": "Brown refers to William Brown, who in 1910, independently of Charles Spearman, derived the Spearman-Brown prophecy formula for the reliability of a lengthened test; he is remembered for reliability work, not for a theory of group factors.", "c": "Charles Spearman (1904) proposed the two-factor theory, a general factor g plus a specific factor s for each test; group factors were what later theorists added against him.", "d": "Edward Thorndike (1927) proposed the multifactor ('anarchic') theory, intelligence as many separate specific abilities measured by his CAVD test (completion, arithmetic, vocabulary, directions); that is not group factor theory."}}, {"q": "According to Allport the main unit of structure is:", "concept": "The answer is b: for Gordon Allport (1937) the main unit of personality structure is the trait, and the trait that really describes a person is the individual trait. In Personality: A Psychological Interpretation, Allport said traits are real structures inside the person (neuropsychic structures) that make many situations functionally equivalent and so produce consistent behaviour. He separated common traits, shared by people of a culture and useful for comparing them, from individual traits, unique to one person. He thought individual traits were the true units, and in 1961 (Pattern and Growth in Personality) he renamed them personal dispositions: cardinal (one ruling passion that colours almost everything, and rare), central (the 5 to 10 traits a friend would write in a reference letter) and secondary (narrow preferences shown only in some situations). Example: Kabir's friends describe him as honest, punctual, witty, stubborn and generous; these are his central dispositions, while his love of filter coffee is a secondary one. Option b is right because, among the options, only 'individual trait' names a unit of structure in Allport's theory. Exam trap: functional autonomy and proprium are also Allport's own terms, so they look right, but one is a principle of motivation and the other is the self. Key note: Allport called the trait, which he later refined as the personal disposition, the basic unit. Since only 'individual trait' is offered, b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Instinct is the unit of William McDougall's (1908) theory of motivation, and Sigmund Freud built on instinctual drives; Allport rejected instincts as an account of adult personality.", "c": "Functional autonomy is Gordon Allport's principle that adult motives become independent of their origins (a sailor who first went to sea for money later loves the sea itself); it explains motivation, not structure.", "d": "Proprium is Gordon Allport's (1955) term for the self, the sense of 'me' that unifies the traits; it is the integrating core, not the unit from which personality is built."}}, {"q": "The types of personality were discussed by:", "concept": "The answer is a: the classic theory of personality types was put forward by Carl Jung (1921). In Psychological Types, Jung described two attitudes, the direction in which psychic energy flows: introversion (towards the inner world of thoughts and feelings) and extraversion (towards people and things). He added four functions: thinking and feeling, which judge (the rational functions), and sensation and intuition, which perceive (the irrational functions). Each attitude combined with a dominant function gives eight types, such as the extraverted thinking type or the introverted feeling type. Jung's scheme became the basis of the Myers-Briggs Type Indicator, built by Katharine Briggs and her daughter Isabel Briggs Myers (published 1962). Example: Riya recharges alone with a book and trusts her hunches (introverted intuitive), while her cousin Arjun loves crowded parties and wants facts he can check (extraverted sensation). A type theory sorts people into separate categories, unlike a trait theory, which places everyone on continuous dimensions. Option a is right because Jung is the theorist whose name textbooks attach to personality types; introversion and extraversion are his terms. Exam trap: Sigmund Freud (1908) wrote on anal character, Ivan Pavlov later sorted dogs' nervous systems into types, and Alfred Adler late in his career named lifestyle types, so all four names look possible. Key note: Freud, Pavlov and Adler each mentioned types in passing, but the classic personality typology in textbooks is Jung's, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Sigmund Freud described oral, anal and phallic character traits that come from fixation at psychosexual stages, but his theory is a dynamic model of id, ego and superego, not a typology.", "c": "Ivan Pavlov described types of nervous system in dogs (strong and excitable, strong and lively, strong and calm, weak), later linked to the old temperaments and used by Hans Eysenck; these are types of nervous activity, not the classic personality types.", "d": "Alfred Adler sketched four lifestyle types (ruling, getting, avoiding and socially useful) late in his work, but his Individual Psychology is about inferiority, striving and social interest, not types."}}, {"q": "Environmental determinants of personality include all of the following kinds of factors except:", "concept": "The answer is d: psychological factors are not environmental determinants of personality; they belong to a separate group of determinants inside the person. Textbooks usually sort the determinants of personality into three groups. Biological determinants: heredity, physique, the endocrine glands and the nervous system. Psychological determinants: intelligence, motives, emotions, attitudes and the self-concept. Environmental determinants: the physical environment (climate, geography), social factors (family, parenting, school, peers, neighbourhood), cultural factors (customs, values, religion) and economic factors (income, social class, opportunities). Birth order, a child's position among brothers and sisters, is a family factor and so part of the social environment. Alfred Adler (1927) made it famous: the firstborn is 'dethroned' by the next baby, the second child tends to be competitive, and the youngest may be pampered. Example: Arjun is the eldest of three in a joint family in Lucknow and takes charge of his siblings (birth order, a social and family factor); his family's modest income shapes his ambitions (economic); his own intelligence and self-confidence are psychological determinants, part of him rather than his surroundings. Option d is right because social factors, economic factors and birth order all come from outside the person, while psychological factors come from within. Exam trap: birth order looks biological because it is fixed at birth, so students pick c; but it matters through the way the family treats each child, which makes it environmental. Key note: the option printed 'Poth order' is a misprint for birth order.", "traps": {"a": "Social factors (family, school, peers, neighbourhood) are the core of the environmental determinants, so they are included, not excluded.", "b": "Economic factors (family income, social class, opportunities) are part of the environment a child grows up in, so they are included.", "c": "Birth order (printed 'Poth order') is the child's position among siblings, a family and therefore social-environmental factor stressed by Alfred Adler, so it is included."}}, {"q": "According to Adler, personality development is generally influenced by reactions to:", "concept": "The answer is d: for Alfred Adler (1907), personality develops mainly as a reaction to feelings of inferiority. In his Study of Organ Inferiority, Adler began with organ inferiority: a person with a weak organ or body part tries to compensate for it, sometimes so well that the weakness becomes a strength (the stammering Demosthenes became a great orator). After breaking with Sigmund Freud in 1911 he founded Individual Psychology and widened the idea. Every child starts small, weak and dependent on adults, so every human being begins with inferiority feelings. These feelings are normal and are the spur to growth. Personality takes shape through compensation, the lifelong striving for superiority (later called striving for perfection or completion), guided by a fictional final goal, by social interest and by a style of life formed in the first 4 to 5 years. If inferiority feelings become overwhelming, the result is an inferiority complex; if a person overcompensates and boasts or dominates, the result is a superiority complex. Example: Kabir, the smallest boy in his class and teased at cricket, practises bowling every evening and becomes the school's best spinner; his feeling of inferiority drove the effort. Option d is right because inferiority feeling is the universal starting point to which personality is the reaction. Exam trap: superiority appears in Adler's theory too, so b tempts; but superiority is the goal people strive towards, not the feeling they are reacting to.", "traps": {"a": "Sensory stimuli are the raw input studied in sensation and perception; Alfred Adler's theory concerns the meaning a child gives to his own weakness, not sensory input.", "b": "Superiority is the goal of striving, what a person moves towards when compensating, and a superiority complex is overcompensation; personality develops in reaction to inferiority, not to superiority feelings.", "c": "'All types of strong feelings' is too broad; Alfred Adler singled out one feeling, inferiority, as the universal starting point of personality development."}}, {"q": "The following is a non-probability sampling:", "concept": "The answer is c: purposive sampling is a non-probability method, because the researcher chooses the cases by judgement rather than by chance. Sampling means selecting a part (a sample) of a population to study. The methods fall into two families. In probability sampling every member of the population has a known, non-zero chance of being chosen, and a random process does the choosing, so sampling error can be estimated and results generalised. Its forms are simple random sampling (lottery or random numbers), systematic sampling (every kth name from a list), stratified random sampling (random draws within strata such as sex or class) and cluster sampling (random choice of whole groups such as schools or villages). In non-probability sampling there is no random process, so each member's chance of selection is unknown. Its forms are purposive or judgement sampling (deliberately picking cases judged typical or informative), quota sampling (filling set numbers for each subgroup by convenience), convenience or incidental sampling (whoever is available) and snowball sampling (participants recruit others). Example: Meera, studying burnout among ICU nurses in a Pune hospital, chooses the six nurses she judges most experienced; that is purposive sampling, and her findings cannot be generalised with known error. Option c is right because purposive selection depends on the researcher's judgement, not on chance. Exam trap: stratified and cluster sampling involve the researcher forming groups, so they seem judgement based, but the selection within or among those groups is random.", "traps": {"a": "Random sampling is the basic probability method: every member has an equal chance of selection, drawn by lottery or random numbers.", "b": "Stratified sampling divides the population into strata (sex, class, region) and draws randomly within each; the random draw makes it a probability method.", "d": "Cluster sampling randomly selects whole natural groups (schools, villages) and studies their members; the random choice of clusters makes it a probability method."}}, {"q": "An attitude scale with five points on it varying from strongly approved to strongly disapproved is known as:", "concept": "The answer is a: a five-point attitude scale running from strongly approve to strongly disapprove is the Likert type. Rensis Likert (1932), in 'A Technique for the Measurement of Attitudes', introduced the method of summated ratings. The respondent reads a set of statements about an attitude object and marks one of five points for each: strongly approve, approve, undecided, disapprove, strongly disapprove (modern versions say agree and disagree). Answers are scored 5 to 1, reversed for unfavourable statements, and the item scores are added to give a total attitude score. Likert's method needs no panel of judges, so it is quicker to build than the scale of Louis Thurstone (1929) and usually just as reliable, which is why it became the most used attitude scale. Example: Arjun's survey on attitudes to mental health includes 'People with depression can recover fully', with five boxes from strongly agree to strongly disagree; Riya's total over 20 such items tells how favourable her attitude is. Option a is right because the five response points with a neutral middle and the summing of scores are the defining features of the Likert scale. Exam trap: Thurstone's scale also uses statements and also has fixed points, but those points belong to the judges' 11-point sorting, not to the respondent, who only ticks the statements he agrees with.", "traps": {"b": "The Thurstone type (Louis Thurstone and Ernest Chave, 1929) uses equal-appearing intervals: judges sort statements on an 11-point scale to give each a scale value, and respondents only tick the statements they agree with.", "c": "The Bogardus type (Emory Bogardus, 1925) is the social distance scale: seven steps from accepting a group as close kin by marriage down to excluding them from one's country.", "d": "The Guttman type (Louis Guttman, 1944) is a cumulative scalogram of items ordered by strength, so agreeing with a strong item implies agreeing with all weaker ones; it tests whether the scale is unidimensional."}}, {"q": "Which of the following statements is true in behavioural science research?", "concept": "The answer is d: all three statements are true of measurement in behavioural science. First, measurement is indirect. Constructs such as intelligence, anxiety or attitude cannot be seen or weighed; we infer them from behaviour, test answers or ratings, through operational definitions. Second, measurement gives only approximate information. Every score contains error. In classical test theory, which grew from the work of Charles Spearman (1904), an observed score equals a true score plus error. The reliability coefficient and the standard error of measurement (SEM) tell us how large the error is: with an SEM of 3, an IQ of 110 is best read as a band of about 104 to 116. Third, many errors arise because we cannot devise a proper unit. Physics has fixed units (the metre, the kilogram) and a true zero. Psychology has neither for most traits: a score of zero on an anxiety scale does not mean no anxiety, and the step from 100 to 110 IQ may not equal the step from 130 to 140. In the scheme of Stanley Smith Stevens (1946), most psychological scores are ordinal, at best interval. Example: Kabir scores 30 and Riya 15 on an anxiety scale; we cannot say Kabir is twice as anxious. Option d is right because each of a, b and c describes a real limitation of psychological measurement. Exam trap: statement b says 'always', and students are taught that absolute words mark a false option; here the absolute is true, since no psychological score is free of error.", "traps": {"a": "True but incomplete: indirectness is only one limitation; choosing it alone ignores measurement error (b) and the lack of a proper unit and zero (c).", "b": "True but incomplete: every score carries error, but measurement is also indirect (a) and lacks fixed units (c), so b alone leaves out two true statements.", "c": "True but incomplete: the unit problem is real, but measurement is also indirect (a) and always approximate (b)."}}, {"q": "The t-test is considered to be very useful because:", "concept": "The answer is c: the t-test is valued because it can be applied even when samples are small. A t-test compares means (one sample against a population value, two independent groups, or the same people tested twice). The older large-sample (z) test uses the normal curve and needs either the population standard deviation or a sample big enough (about 30 or more) for the sample standard deviation to be a stable estimate. With small samples that estimate is unstable, and the ratio no longer follows the normal curve. William Sealy Gosset (1908), a chemist at the Guinness brewery in Dublin, had to judge barley and stout from tiny batches. He worked out the exact distribution of this ratio and, because Guinness barred staff from publishing under their own names, signed his Biometrika paper 'Student': hence Student's t. The t distribution is a family of curves, one for each number of degrees of freedom (df). It is bell-shaped like the normal curve but has heavier tails, so larger values are needed for significance: at the .05 level (two-tailed) the critical t with 10 df is 2.23, against 1.96 for z. As df grows, t approaches the normal curve. Example: Meera tests a relaxation technique on 8 students against 8 controls; with so few cases she uses a t-test with 14 df. Option c is right because Gosset designed the t distribution precisely for small samples. Exam trap: option a is technically true, since an independent t-test can handle unequal group sizes, but the z-test can too, so it is not what made the t-test special. Key note: option a is literally true, but textbooks give the use of small samples as the reason the t-test is so valuable, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "An independent-samples t-test can handle unequal group sizes, but so can the z-test and ANOVA, and with unequal sizes and unequal variances Welch's correction (Bernard Welch, 1947) is needed; unequal size is not why the t-test is prized.", "b": "No significance test ensures correct rejection; every decision carries a risk of Type I error (rejecting a true null hypothesis, set by alpha) or Type II error (missing a real effect).", "d": "The t-test assumes random sampling (independent observations) and roughly normal populations; non-random samples undermine it, so this describes a violation, not a strength."}}, {"q": "In achievement test which type of validity is required?", "concept": "The answer is d: an achievement test above all needs content validity, so that its items fairly represent what was taught. Validity is the extent to which a test measures what it claims to measure. Its main kinds are content, criterion-related (concurrent and predictive) and construct validity; face validity is a non-technical extra. An achievement test measures how much a student has learned of a defined course, so its validity depends on how well its items sample the syllabus and the instructional objectives. Content validity is built in at the planning stage with a blueprint, or table of specifications: a two-way chart of content areas against objectives (knowledge, understanding, application, as in the taxonomy of Benjamin Bloom, 1956), with the number of items in each cell in proportion to the weight of that topic. Subject experts judge it by comparing items with the syllabus; no correlation coefficient is involved. Example: Arjun's Class 10 science teacher spends 40 percent of the year on chemistry, so about 40 percent of the test marks should be chemistry; a paper full of physics questions would lack content validity however reliable it was. Option d is right because an achievement test looks back at a defined body of teaching, and only content validity checks that coverage. Exam trap: face validity is also judged by looking at the items, so students confuse the two; but face validity is how the test appears to the takers, while content validity is a systematic expert check against the syllabus.", "traps": {"a": "Face validity is only how relevant the test looks to the people taking it; it helps cooperation but is not evidence that the items cover the syllabus.", "b": "Concurrent validity correlates the test with a criterion measured at the same time (a new anxiety scale against a psychiatrist's present rating); it suits diagnostic tests rather than achievement tests.", "c": "Predictive validity shows how well a test forecasts a future criterion, as when an aptitude or entrance test predicts later grades; achievement tests look back at what was taught, not forward."}}, {"q": "In which conditions validity depends on reliability?", "concept": "The answer is c: validity depends on reliability in both heterogeneous and homogeneous tests. Reliability is consistency of scores; validity is accuracy for a stated purpose. A test can be reliable without being valid, but it cannot be valid without being reliable, because an unreliable score is largely random error, and random error correlates with nothing. Formally, a test's validity coefficient cannot exceed the square root of its reliability coefficient (the index of reliability): if reliability is 0.64, validity can be at most 0.80. Charles Spearman's (1904) correction for attenuation shows how much unreliability lowers an observed correlation. A homogeneous test has items that all measure one trait (a 30-item arithmetic speed test), so its internal consistency is high. A heterogeneous test samples several abilities or topics (a general knowledge quiz or a broad achievement battery), so its items correlate only modestly with each other. In both, scores that change from one day to the next cannot predict anything well. Example: Kabir's spelling test (homogeneous) and Riya's general knowledge quiz (heterogeneous) both give very different scores on two Mondays; neither can then predict school marks, whatever their content. Option c is right because the ceiling that reliability places on validity holds for every kind of test. Exam trap: students link reliability only to homogeneous tests, because internal consistency is easiest to see there, and so pick b. Key note: the link is tighter for homogeneous tests. In a heterogeneous test internal consistency can be modest while validity is good, and pushing homogeneity too high can narrow the content and even lower validity (Jane Loevinger's 1954 'attenuation paradox'). But reliability in the sense of stable scores still limits validity in both kinds of test, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "True for heterogeneous tests, but only half the answer: reliability also caps validity in homogeneous tests, so a alone leaves out b.", "b": "True for homogeneous tests, where the link is closest, but reliability also limits validity in heterogeneous tests, so b alone leaves out a.", "d": "'None of these' cannot be right, because validity depends on reliability in every test (it cannot exceed the square root of reliability); the correct choice is c, both kinds of test."}}, {"q": "Which of the following approaches to psychology does not consider the individual's conscious experience?", "concept": "The answer is a: the behavioural approach does not consider the individual's conscious experience. John B. Watson (1913), in 'Psychology as the behaviorist views it', declared that psychology should be the objective study of observable behaviour, with the goal of predicting and controlling it. Consciousness and introspection, the method of the structuralist laboratories, were thrown out as unscientific, because no one else can check what a person reports seeing inside his own mind. Behaviour was explained by stimuli, responses and conditioning. B. F. Skinner (1938) carried this on as radical behaviourism: behaviour is explained by its consequences (reinforcement history), and the mind is treated as a 'black box' that adds nothing to the explanation. Example: Meera wants to stop biting her nails. A behaviourist counts how often she bites, finds the situations that trigger it and sets up a reward chart; he does not ask what she feels or thinks while biting. The other approaches all bring experience or mental processes back in. Option a is right because rejecting conscious experience was the founding principle of behaviourism. Exam trap: social learning theory grew out of behaviourism, so students lump the two together; but Albert Bandura (1977) added cognitive processes such as attention, memory and expectation, so it does consider mental experience.", "traps": {"b": "The humanistic approach (Carl Rogers, 1951; Abraham Maslow, 1954) puts subjective conscious experience, the person's own view of self and world, at its very centre.", "c": "The cognitive approach (Ulric Neisser, 1967) studies mental processes such as perception, attention, memory and thinking, the inner events behaviourism left out.", "d": "Albert Bandura's social learning theory (1977) kept learning from behaviourism but added cognition: attention to the model, memory, expectancies and self-efficacy."}}, {"q": "The following is not included in the process of socialization:", "concept": "The answer is c: maturation is not part of socialisation, because it is biological unfolding rather than learning from society. Socialisation is the lifelong process by which a person learns the norms, values, roles, skills and behaviour patterns of his society and makes them his own. Primary socialisation happens in the family; secondary socialisation continues through school, peers, the media, religion and work. It includes social development: learning to make friends, cooperate and take social roles. It includes prosocial behaviour: sharing, helping and comforting, taught through reinforcement and modelling. It also includes patterns of aggression: in the Bobo doll study of Albert Bandura, Dorothea Ross and Sheila Ross (1961), children who watched an adult hit an inflatable doll later copied the same acts, so aggressive styles are learned from social models, and each culture teaches when and how aggression is allowed. Maturation, by contrast, is the unfolding of inherited, biologically timed changes in the body and nervous system, such as teething, walking and puberty; Arnold Gesell (1929) showed its power in his co-twin study of stair climbing. Maturation provides readiness for learning but is not taught by anyone. Example: Riya learns to greet elders with namaste and to share her tiffin (socialisation); her milk teeth falling out at six is maturation. Option c is right because socialisation covers everything learned from society, and maturation is not learned. Exam trap: aggression sounds antisocial, so students think learning aggression cannot be part of socialisation and pick d; but socialisation transmits all of a society's behaviour patterns, aggressive ones included.", "traps": {"a": "Social development, learning to relate, cooperate and take social roles, is a central outcome of socialisation through family, school and peers.", "b": "Prosocial behaviour (sharing, helping, comforting) is taught by parents and teachers through reinforcement and modelling, a core goal of socialisation.", "d": "Patterns of aggression are learned too: Albert Bandura, Dorothea Ross and Sheila Ross (1961) showed that children copy an adult model's aggression, and cultures teach when aggression is acceptable."}}, {"q": "A dyad consists of:", "concept": "The answer is b: a dyad is a group of two persons. The word comes from the Greek for \"two\", and the German sociologist Georg Simmel (1908) made the dyad and the triad (three persons) basic units in his study of how the number of members shapes group life. Option b is right because a dyad is, by definition, the smallest possible group: two people who interact and depend on each other, such as a married couple, two close friends, or a counsellor and client. Simmel noticed two special features. First, a dyad depends on both members: if one leaves, the group ends, so each person feels directly responsible for it. Second, it is intimate, since there is no audience and no third person to take sides. Add a third person and the triad brings new roles: a mediator, a member who gains from the other two quarrelling, or a coalition of two against one. For example, Riya and Kabir are a dyad as a newly married couple; when their daughter is born they become a triad, and their arguments can now draw the child in. Social psychologists use the same idea for dyadic interaction, such as studies of two strangers getting acquainted. Exam trap: dyad (two) is easily swapped with triad (three); remember \"duo\" and \"dual\" for two, \"tri\" for three. Numbers such as ten or twenty describe small groups or classes, which have no special name of this kind.", "traps": {"a": "Three persons make a triad, Georg Simmel's term for the next group size, where coalitions and mediators become possible; a dyad has only two.", "c": "Ten persons form an ordinary small group (a committee, a cricket squad); it has no special name like dyad and has far more than two members.", "d": "Twenty persons form a larger group such as a class; the size terms dyad and triad stop at two and three."}}, {"q": "As compared to a democratic leader, the authoritarian leader:", "concept": "The answer is a: compared with a democratic leader, the authoritarian leader wields more absolute power. The classic evidence is the leadership climates study of Kurt Lewin, Ronald Lippitt and Ralph White (1939). Adults led after-school clubs of ten-year-old boys in three styles. The authoritarian (autocratic) leader made every decision alone and gave orders one step at a time, so the boys rarely knew the larger plan. The democratic leader let the group discuss and choose, and explained the overall goal. The laissez-faire leader left the boys to themselves. Under autocratic leaders the boys worked hard only while watched, stopped when the leader left the room, and became either hostile (with scapegoating of one boy) or apathetic. Democratic groups were friendlier and more \"we\" minded, kept working without the leader, and the boys liked this style best. Laissez-faire groups got little done. Option a is right because power is the defining difference: the autocrat holds authority alone, while the democrat shares it with the group. Imagine Arjun, a project head who sets every deadline and task himself and never consults the team; he holds more absolute power than Meera, who lets her team vote on the plan. Exam trap: autocratic leaders can get high output in the short run, so students assume they also make goals clearer and the group more united; Lewin's findings show the reverse on both counts, which also rules out \"All of the above\".", "traps": {"b": "Clear group goals came from democratic leaders, who discussed and explained the overall plan; Lewin's autocrats gave orders one step at a time, leaving future steps uncertain to the boys.", "c": "Cohesiveness was higher under democratic leadership in the Lewin, Lippitt and White (1939) study; autocratic groups showed more hostility, scapegoating or apathy.", "d": "All of the above fails because only option a is true: autocrats hold more power but neither clarify goals nor raise cohesiveness."}}, {"q": "Social psychology:", "concept": "The answer is a: social psychology emphasises the individual or small group. The classic definition is that of Gordon Allport (1954): social psychology studies how the thoughts, feelings and behaviour of individuals are influenced by the actual, imagined or implied presence of others. The unit of analysis is the person in a social situation, along with the small, face-to-face groups (a family, a team, a jury) that the person belongs to. Earlier, his brother Floyd Allport (1924) argued that there is no psychology of groups that is not, in the end, a psychology of individuals. So social psychology asks why Kabir speaks freely among friends but stays silent in a lecture hall, or why Meera gives a wrong answer when the classmates before her all give it, as in the conformity studies of Solomon Asch (1951). Option a is right because social psychology is a branch of psychology, and its focus is how one person thinks, feels and acts among others. By contrast, sociology studies society, its institutions and social classes as wholes, and anthropology studies whole cultures, often through long fieldwork. Social psychology borrows from both, and a sociological social psychology tradition (symbolic interactionism) exists, yet the field belongs to psychology. Exam trap: because social psychology talks about groups, culture and society, students pick \"studies the culture and society\" or call it a branch of sociology; those describe neighbouring disciplines. Key note: some older texts call social psychology a bridge between psychology and sociology, but its focus on the individual is what separates it from sociology, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Studying culture and society as wholes is the work of sociology and cultural anthropology; social psychology looks at the individual within those settings.", "c": "Social psychology is a branch of psychology, not of sociology or anthropology, even though it overlaps with sociology through sociological social psychology.", "d": "All of the above fails because b and c describe sociology and anthropology; only a is true of social psychology."}}, {"q": "Abnormal psychology is concerned with:", "concept": "The answer is d: abnormal psychology is concerned with understanding, treatment and prevention of mental disorders, so all of the above apply. Abnormal psychology is the branch of psychology that studies abnormal behaviour and experience: anxiety, depression, schizophrenia, substance use and the rest. Like psychology in general, it has broad aims: to describe disorders (symptoms, course, how common they are), to explain them, to treat them and to prevent them. Understanding (option a) covers description and aetiology, the study of causes, using biological, psychological and social factors together in the biopsychosocial and diathesis-stress views. Treatment (option b) covers the therapies that grow from that understanding: medication, psychotherapy, family work and rehabilitation. Prevention (option c) is the newest aim; Gerald Caplan (1964) set out three levels. Primary prevention stops new cases (stress management in schools), secondary prevention catches cases early (screening), and tertiary prevention reduces disability in established cases (rehabilitation). Option d is right because the field covers all three. Take Arjun, a student who starts having panic attacks before exams. Abnormal psychology explains why (a learned fear of his own bodily sensations), treats it (cognitive behaviour therapy) and asks how such attacks could be prevented in other students (exam stress workshops). Exam trap: students often think abnormal psychology only describes and explains, leaving treatment to clinical psychology; in textbook definitions treatment and prevention are part of its scope too, so any single option is incomplete.", "traps": {"a": "Understanding (description and causes) is one aim of abnormal psychology, but choosing it alone leaves out treatment and prevention.", "b": "Treatment is part of abnormal psychology's scope, but alone it misses understanding the causes and preventing disorders.", "c": "Prevention (Gerald Caplan's primary, secondary and tertiary levels) is included, but alone it omits understanding and treatment."}}, {"q": "Phobias originate from:", "concept": "The answer is d: in the classic textbook account, phobias originate from unconscious motivation. A phobia is an intense, persistent and irrational fear of a specific object or situation (heights, dogs, closed spaces) that leads to avoidance. Sigmund Freud's case of Little Hans (1909) gave the psychoanalytic explanation. Hans, a five-year-old Viennese boy, was terrified that a horse would bite him. Freud argued that his real fear was of his father, his rival in the Oedipal conflict; because this fear was unacceptable, it was repressed and displaced onto horses, which could simply be avoided. On this view a phobia is a defence: anxiety from an unconscious conflict is attached to an outside object. Option d is right because, among these choices, only unconscious motivation names a cause of phobias in a recognised theory. Phobic people keep contact with reality, usually know their fear is excessive and do not hallucinate; those features belong to psychosis. Learning theory gives the main alternative: John B. Watson and Rosalie Rayner (1920) conditioned fear in Little Albert, Joseph Wolpe and Stanley Rachman (1960) reread Little Hans as fear conditioned after he saw a horse fall, and Martin Seligman (1971) added preparedness. If Meera has feared lifts since childhood but cannot say why, a psychoanalyst would look for a hidden conflict. Exam trap: \"unfounded beliefs\" sounds right because phobic fear is irrational, but it describes the fear, not where it comes from. Key note: modern cognitive accounts do stress catastrophic beliefs, but these are seen mainly as keeping a phobia going; as an origin among these options the classic textbook answer is unconscious motivation, so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Loss of touch with reality marks psychosis (schizophrenia, delusional disorder); people with phobias keep reality contact and usually know their fear is excessive.", "b": "Hallucinations (perceptions without a real stimulus) are psychotic symptoms; a phobia is an exaggerated fear of a real object, not a false perception.", "c": "Unfounded beliefs describe how irrational phobic fear is, and cognitive models say such beliefs keep phobias going, but they are not the textbook origin offered here; unconscious motivation is."}}, {"q": "In anxiety neurosis the eliciting stimuli is not:", "concept": "The answer is a: in anxiety neurosis the eliciting stimulus is not specific. Anxiety neurosis is an older diagnostic name, introduced by Sigmund Freud (1895) when he separated it from neurasthenia; today it falls mainly under generalised anxiety disorder (GAD) and panic disorder. Its mark is free-floating anxiety: a constant state of worry and tension that is not tied to any particular object or situation. The person feels apprehensive about health, money, work and family in turn, with restlessness, muscle tension, poor sleep and poor concentration. Because the source of the anxiety cannot be pinned down, textbooks describe its eliciting stimuli as obscure (hidden, unclear), complex (many interacting worries) and pervasive (spread across most of life). Option a is right because a specific eliciting stimulus is exactly what anxiety neurosis lacks. Compare Meera and Arjun. Meera feels tense all day and cannot say why; one worry slides into the next. That is anxiety neurosis. Arjun is calm until he sees a dog, then panics and crosses the road. His fear has a specific trigger, which makes it a phobia. Exam trap: the stem is a negative (\"is not\"), so the three options that do fit anxiety neurosis (obscure, complex, pervasive) are the distractors; the odd one out, \"specific\", is the defining feature of phobic disorders.", "traps": {"b": "Obscure is true of anxiety neurosis: the source of free-floating anxiety is hidden or unclear to the sufferer, so it is not the exception asked for.", "c": "Complex is true of anxiety neurosis: many interacting worries feed the anxiety, so it fits rather than being the exception.", "d": "Quite pervasive is true: the anxiety spreads across most areas of life, the hallmark of free-floating anxiety, so it is not the exception."}}, {"q": "A measure of variability is to ______ as a measure of central tendency is to ______.", "concept": "The answer is c: standard deviation is to variability as the mean is to central tendency. Descriptive statistics summarise a set of scores in two main ways. A measure of central tendency gives one typical value: the mean (the arithmetic average), the median (the middle score) and the mode (the most frequent score). A measure of variability (dispersion) shows how spread out the scores are around that centre: the range, the quartile deviation, the mean deviation, the variance and the standard deviation (SD). The SD is the square root of the average squared deviation from the mean; Karl Pearson (1894) introduced the term. The mean and the SD are the standard partners, reported together in almost every research paper. Option c is right because it is the only pair that gives a variability measure first and a central tendency measure second, as the stem requires. Suppose Riya's class and Kabir's class both have a mean of 60 marks, but Riya's class has an SD of 5 and Kabir's an SD of 15: the averages match, yet Kabir's class is far more spread out. Exam trap: in analogy items examiners offer pairs that belong together but do not match the stem's pattern; mode and median are both central tendency, correlation and scatter plot are both about relationships, and histogram and frequency polygon are both graphs.", "traps": {"a": "Mode and median are both measures of central tendency, so neither fills the variability slot.", "b": "Correlation and the scatter plot describe the relationship between two variables (one as a coefficient, the other as a graph), not spread or the typical score.", "d": "Histogram and frequency polygon are two graphs of a frequency distribution, not measures of variability or central tendency."}}, {"q": "One student in the class got an extremely low score of 10 out of 100 on a test. Which measure of central tendency is most affected by this low score?", "concept": "The answer is a: the mean is the measure of central tendency most affected by one extremely low score. The mean is calculated from the value of every score: add them all and divide by the number of scores. So a single extreme score (an outlier) pulls the mean towards itself. The median is the middle score when scores are put in order; it depends only on position, so an extreme score at one end hardly moves it. The mode is the most frequent score and is not touched by a single unusual value. Option a is right because only the mean uses the actual size of every score. Take five students with marks of 60, 65, 70, 75 and 80. The mean is 70 and the median is 70. Now suppose Arjun's 60 is replaced by a 10: the marks are 10, 65, 70, 75 and 80. The mean drops to 60, but the median stays at 70. This is why the median is preferred for skewed data such as family income, where a few very rich households inflate the mean. Exam trap: range is even more affected by an extreme score (here it jumps from 20 to 70), but range is a measure of variability, not of central tendency, so it does not answer the stem.", "traps": {"b": "The median depends only on the middle position of the ordered scores, so one extreme score barely changes it; that is why it suits skewed data.", "c": "The mode is the most frequent score; a single unusual score of 10 does not change which score occurs most often.", "d": "Range (highest minus lowest) is badly affected by extremes, but it is a measure of variability, not of central tendency."}}, {"q": "For his class presentation, Liam prepared a graph that depicted a frequency distribution with vertical bars that touched each other. Liam has constructed a:", "concept": "The answer is c: a graph of a frequency distribution with vertical bars that touch each other is a histogram. To build one, scores are grouped into class intervals (say 30 to 39, 40 to 49, 50 to 59) marked along the horizontal axis, with frequency on the vertical axis. Over each interval a rectangle is drawn whose height equals the number of scores in it. The bars touch because the score scale is continuous: one interval ends exactly where the next begins. The word histogram is usually credited to Karl Pearson in the 1890s. Option c is right because touching bars over a frequency distribution are the defining look of a histogram. If Kabir plots the marks of 40 classmates this way, the bars show at a glance where most marks fall and whether the distribution is skewed. Related graphs differ in small ways. Join the midpoints of the bar tops with straight lines, close the line on the baseline, and you get a frequency polygon, useful for overlaying two distributions. Separate bars with gaps form a bar graph, used for categories such as religion or state of birth. Exam trap: students confuse the histogram with the bar graph; gaps mean separate categories, touching bars mean continuous class intervals.", "traps": {"a": "A scatter diagram plots pairs of scores as dots to show the correlation between two variables; it has no bars.", "b": "A frequency polygon joins the midpoints of the class intervals with straight lines; it uses points and lines, not touching bars.", "d": "Standard deviation is a statistic measuring spread, not a graph at all."}}, {"q": "Range is to mode as ______ is to ______.", "concept": "The answer is d: range is to mode as variability is to central tendency. This analogy asks what each term is an example of. The range is the simplest measure of variability: the highest score minus the lowest score. It shows how widely scores are spread but depends only on the two extreme scores. The mode is the simplest measure of central tendency: the score that occurs most often, and the only average that can be used with nominal data such as favourite colour. So the relation is \"example of a category\": range belongs to variability as mode belongs to central tendency. Option d is right because it names the two categories in the same order as the stem. The other members of each family follow the same pattern: interquartile range, variance and standard deviation for variability; mean and median for central tendency. For Meera's test scores of 40, 55, 55, 60 and 90, the range is 50 and the mode is 55. Exam trap: option b looks tempting because mode appears in it, but median and mode are both central tendency, so the pair does not mirror the stem; options a and c pair ideas from correlation and standard scores.", "traps": {"a": "Correlation and scatter diagram are two ways of showing the relationship between two variables (a coefficient and its graph), not the categories that range and mode belong to.", "b": "Median and mode are both measures of central tendency, so the pair does not mirror range (variability) to mode (central tendency).", "c": "The correlation coefficient measures relationship and the z score shows a score's position in SD units; neither is the category of range or of mode."}}, {"q": "In a book entitled \"Choosing a Vocation\" who has described a three-part model for career counseling?", "concept": "The answer is c: Frank Parsons described the three-part model of career counselling in Choosing a Vocation (1909). Parsons, a Boston social reformer, opened the Vocation Bureau in Boston in 1908 to help young people find suitable work; he died later that year, and his book was published after his death. It made him the father of vocational guidance. His model, later called the trait-and-factor approach, has three steps: (1) a clear understanding of yourself, your aptitudes, abilities, interests, ambitions, resources and limitations; (2) knowledge of the requirements, conditions of success, advantages, pay and prospects of different kinds of work; and (3) \"true reasoning\" on the relations between these two groups of facts, that is, matching the person to the job. Later aptitude and interest tests were built to supply the first step scientifically. Option c is right because both the book title and the three-part model are Parsons's. A counsellor using his method with Kabir would assess his aptitudes and interests, show him what engineering, teaching and design jobs actually demand, then reason with him about the best fit. Exam trap: Samuel Gladding (Counseling: A Comprehensive Profession, 1988) and Jeffrey Kottler (On Being a Therapist, 1986) are modern counselling authors whose textbooks describe Parsons's model, so their names feel familiar in the same chapter; neither wrote Choosing a Vocation.", "traps": {"a": "Samuel Gladding wrote the modern textbook Counseling: A Comprehensive Profession, which reports Parsons's model; he did not write Choosing a Vocation.", "b": "Jeffrey Kottler is a modern writer on counselling and on therapists' lives (On Being a Therapist); the 1909 three-part model is not his.", "d": "John B. Watson founded behaviourism (1913); he was not a vocational guidance pioneer and did not write Choosing a Vocation."}}, {"q": "An activity that is designed to work primarily with those who are experiencing developmental or adjustment problems is called:", "concept": "The answer is d: an activity designed to work mainly with people experiencing developmental or adjustment problems is counselling. Counselling is a professional, face-to-face relationship in which a trained counsellor helps a basically healthy person understand a problem of living and reach his or her own decision. Its clients are in the normal range: a student choosing a stream, a new employee struggling to fit in, a couple adjusting to a first baby, someone grieving a loss. The work is usually brief and focused on the present, on growth, prevention and coping. Psychotherapy, by contrast, works with more serious or long-standing disorders (severe depression, personality disorders) and seeks deeper and slower personality change. The two overlap so much that Carl Rogers (1942, Counseling and Psychotherapy) used the words almost interchangeably, but counselling textbooks draw the line by the severity of the problem and the depth of the goals. Option d is right because developmental and adjustment problems are the defining territory of counselling. If Riya, a first-year hostel student, feels homesick and cannot settle into college life, a college counsellor rather than a psychotherapist is the natural first help. Exam trap: psychotherapy is the near-miss, since both are talking treatments; the clue words \"developmental\" and \"adjustment\" point to counselling, while \"disorder\" or \"personality change\" point to psychotherapy.", "traps": {"a": "Psychotherapy works mainly with more serious or long-standing disorders and aims at deeper personality change, not routine developmental or adjustment problems.", "b": "Social work addresses social and environmental needs (welfare, family services, community resources) and is not defined by work on developmental or adjustment problems.", "c": "Treatment is a broad medical-model word for curing illness; it is not the name of the helping activity for normal developmental problems."}}, {"q": "To enable clients to pay close attention to their moment-to-moment experiences so they can recognize and integrate disowned aspects of themselves is the goal of:", "concept": "The answer is a: helping clients attend to moment-to-moment experience so they can recognise and integrate disowned parts of themselves is the goal of Gestalt counselling. Gestalt therapy was developed by Fritz Perls with Laura Perls and set out in Gestalt Therapy (1951), written by Fritz Perls, Ralph Hefferline and Paul Goodman. It borrows the Gestalt idea of the whole: a healthy person is an integrated whole, but people split off feelings and needs they cannot accept (\"I am never angry\"). These disowned parts show up as unfinished business, tension and blocked awareness. The therapist keeps the client in the here and now, asking \"what\" and \"how\" (\"What are you feeling right now? How are you holding your hands?\") rather than \"why\". Techniques include the empty chair, in which the client speaks to an imagined person or to a part of the self; two-chair dialogue between \"top dog\" and \"underdog\"; and owning statements (\"I choose\", \"I take responsibility for\"). Option a is right because awareness and integration are exactly the stated aims of Gestalt work. If Meera insists she is not hurt by her father, a Gestalt counsellor might ask her to speak to him in the empty chair and to notice her clenched fists as she does. Exam trap: the word Gestalt also names the perception school of Max Wertheimer (1912); the therapy shares the idea of wholeness but is a separate humanistic approach.", "traps": {"b": "Reality counselling (William Glasser, 1965) focuses on present behaviour, responsible choices and meeting basic needs, not on awareness of disowned feelings.", "c": "Adlerian counselling (Alfred Adler's individual psychology) explores lifestyle, social interest, birth order and mistaken goals, using encouragement rather than moment-to-moment awareness work.", "d": "Cognitive counselling (Aaron Beck) identifies and changes distorted thoughts and beliefs; it works on thinking, not on reowning split-off parts of experience."}}, {"q": "Family counseling bears some similarity to ______ counseling in that the systematic dynamics are as important as individual behavior.", "concept": "The answer is a: family counselling resembles group counselling because, in both, the dynamics of the system matter as much as each person's behaviour. Family counselling grew from systems thinking from the 1950s onwards, through the work of Murray Bowen (family systems theory), Virginia Satir (Conjoint Family Therapy, 1964) and Salvador Minuchin (Families and Family Therapy, 1974). Its central idea is that a family is a system: members influence one another in circular loops, so a child's problem may express the family's patterns rather than a flaw in the child alone. Group counselling, with several clients and one or two leaders, also works through interaction. Members' roles, alliances, norms and conflicts inside the group become material for change, and Irvin Yalom (1970) described therapeutic factors such as universality and cohesiveness that arise only from group life. Option a is right because group counselling is the other format in which the counsellor treats interaction patterns among several people, not just one individual. Picture Kabir, a teenager who skips school. An individual counsellor studies Kabir; a family counsellor notices that his truancy is the only thing that makes his quarrelling parents work together, much as a group leader notices who speaks for whom. Exam trap: couple counselling also involves more than one person, but it is itself a form of family work with two people; the textbook comparison is with group counselling. The printed \"systematic\" means systemic (to do with systems).", "traps": {"b": "Couple counselling also works with more than one person, but it is a form of family work with two partners, not the separate format the comparison names.", "c": "Individual counselling works with one client alone, so there are no member-to-member dynamics to treat.", "d": "Sex counselling (sex therapy in the tradition of William Masters and Virginia Johnson) targets sexual dysfunction, usually with a couple; it is not defined by system dynamics among several members."}}, {"q": "Guidance as a life-long process is one of the:", "concept": "The answer is b: \"guidance is a life-long process\" is one of the principles of guidance. Guidance is the help given to a person to understand himself or herself and to make wise educational, vocational and personal choices. The classic list of its principles, the basic rules that should run through any guidance programme, comes from Lester D. Crow and Alice Crow (An Introduction to Guidance, 1951) and is repeated in most Indian guidance texts. One of these principles is that guidance should be a continuing process of service from early childhood through adulthood, not a one-time event. The others: it is for all, not only for problem cases; it respects individual differences and the dignity of each person; it is cooperative, never forced; it deals with the whole person (intellectual, emotional, social, physical); and it is the joint task of teachers, parents and counsellors. These differ from the goals of guidance (what it tries to achieve: self-understanding, adjustment, sound decisions) and from its classification into types (educational, vocational, personal and social guidance). Option b is right because life-long continuity is a rule about how guidance must be carried out, which is what a principle is. Arjun needs guidance when choosing his Class 11 stream, again when picking a college course, and again when changing jobs at 35: the help continues through life. Exam trap: goals and principles are easily mixed up; goals name the outcome, principles name the rules of practice. Key note: some texts, such as the NCERT guidance module, discuss the principles and assumptions of guidance under one heading, which is why option c tempts; the standard lists, from Crow and Crow onwards, name life-long continuity as a principle, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Goals of guidance are the outcomes it aims for (self-understanding, adjustment, decision making); being life-long describes how guidance works, not what it aims to achieve.", "c": "Assumptions are the beliefs guidance rests on (every individual can grow, people need help at times); some texts discuss principles and assumptions together, but the standard lists (Crow and Crow) name continuity as a principle.", "d": "Classification sorts guidance into types such as educational, vocational and personal-social; life-long continuity is not a type."}}, {"q": "Choosing a vocation is one of the major concerns of:", "concept": "The answer is d: choosing a vocation is a major concern of vocational guidance. Vocational guidance is the process of helping a person choose an occupation, prepare for it, enter it and progress in it. It began with Frank Parsons, who opened the Vocation Bureau in Boston in 1908 and whose book Choosing a Vocation (1909) gave the field its three steps: know yourself, know the world of work, and match the two by true reasoning. Modern vocational guidance adds aptitude and interest testing, career information and theories of career choice and development, such as the RIASEC types of John Holland (1959) and the life stages of Donald Super (1953). Educational guidance, a term usually traced to the 1914 dissertation of Truman Kelley, helps pupils choose subjects, courses and institutions and handle study problems. Personal and social guidance deals with emotional problems, relationships and adjustment in family and community. Option d is right because selecting an occupation is the defining aim of vocational guidance, just as the title of Parsons's book says. When Meera, finishing her degree, is unsure whether to become a teacher or a nurse, a counsellor who assesses her interests and explains each job's demands is giving vocational guidance. Exam trap: educational and vocational guidance overlap, since choosing a course often leads to a career, so students pick educational guidance; but the choice of a job itself belongs to vocational guidance.", "traps": {"a": "Educational guidance helps with choosing subjects, courses and schools and with study problems; the choice of an occupation belongs to vocational guidance.", "b": "Social guidance helps a person get along with others and adjust in family and community; it does not centre on choosing a job.", "c": "Personal guidance deals with emotional problems, self-understanding and adjustment, not with the choice of a vocation."}}, {"q": "A useful tool for gathering information about family relationships and structures is known as:", "concept": "The answer is a: a genogram is a useful tool for gathering information about family relationships and structures. A genogram is a family tree diagram covering at least three generations. Standard symbols show males as squares and females as circles, with lines for marriages, separations, divorces and children, and marks for deaths, illnesses, addictions and the quality of relationships (close, distant, conflicted, cut off). Murray Bowen made it a core tool of his family systems therapy, using it to trace patterns passed down the generations, and Monica McGoldrick and Randy Gerson standardised it in Genograms in Family Assessment (1985). Counsellors usually draw it with the client in early sessions, and patterns such as repeated divorces or heavy drinking across generations become visible on one page. Option a is right because the genogram is designed specifically to map family structure and relationships. If Kabir, who struggles with anger, draws his genogram, he may notice that his father and grandfather were also described as short-tempered and distant from their sons. Exam trap: sociometry also draws a map of relationships, but of a group (who chooses whom in a class), not of a family across generations. Key note: the option printed \"Grogram\" is a misprint for genogram; read as genogram, a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Sociometry (Jacob L. Moreno, Who Shall Survive?, 1934) maps attractions and rejections within a group as a sociogram, not family structure across generations.", "c": "The TAT (Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan, 1935) is a projective test in which clients tell stories about ambiguous pictures; it reveals needs and conflicts, not a chart of family structure.", "d": "The NEO-FFI (Paul Costa and Robert McCrae) is a 60-item short inventory of the Big Five personality traits; it measures one person, not family relationships."}}, {"q": "Which one of the following is not an ethical issue in counseling?", "concept": "The answer is b: deception is not one of the standard ethical issues in counselling. Counselling ethics codes, such as the American Counseling Association (ACA) Code of Ethics (2014), and counselling textbooks group the issues under familiar headings. Confidentiality: what the client says stays private, with limits such as danger to self or others (the Tarasoff ruling of 1976 set a duty to protect) and child abuse. Client rights and their violation: informed consent, the right to refuse or end counselling, and freedom from exploitation and dual relationships (for example, counselling a friend or one's own student). Technology use: online counselling, email, social media and electronic records, which the 2014 ACA code covers in a section of its own. Deception, deliberately misleading people about the purpose of a procedure, is the classic issue of research ethics. Stanley Milgram's (1963) obedience study led participants to believe they were giving real shocks, and research codes now allow deception only when it is necessary and followed by debriefing. Option b is right because it is the only option that is a research-ethics heading rather than a counselling-ethics heading. If Riya's counsellor posts details of her case on social media, that breaches confidentiality and is a technology-use problem. Exam trap: \"violation of rights\" can sound like a legal rather than an ethical term, but client rights are a core ethics topic. Key note: deceiving a client would of course be wrong, but deception as a named ethical issue belongs to research ethics, while the other three are standard counselling ethics headings, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Confidentiality is the central ethical duty in counselling, with limits such as danger to self or others, so it is an ethical issue, not the exception.", "c": "Violation of client rights (informed consent, freedom from exploitation and dual relationships) is a core counselling ethics topic, so it is not the exception.", "d": "Technology use (online counselling, social media, electronic records) has its own section in the ACA Code of Ethics (2014), so it is a recognised counselling ethics issue."}}, {"q": "An attitude scale which measures only a single trait is called:", "concept": "The answer is c: an attitude scale that measures only a single trait (a unidimensional scale) is the Guttman scale. Louis Guttman (1944) developed scalogram analysis to build a cumulative scale. Statements are arranged from mild to strong so that a person who agrees with a stronger statement should also agree with every weaker one. On a scale of acceptance of a de-addiction centre, for example, the items might read \"in my city\", \"in my locality\" and \"on my street\". If Kabir accepts it on his street, he should accept it in his locality and city too, so his total score tells exactly which items he endorsed. This only works if all items lie on one dimension; the coefficient of reproducibility, which should be 0.90 or more, tests that. Option c is right because Guttman's method is designed to test whether a scale measures one trait only. The other classic scales differ. Rensis Likert's (1932) summated ratings ask for agreement on a five-point scale and add the ratings. The equal-appearing intervals scale of Louis Thurstone and Ernest Chave (1929) uses judges to give statements scale values. The social distance scale of Emory Bogardus (1925) measures how close a relation one would accept with members of a group. Exam trap: Likert scales are often treated as unidimensional in practice, but only Guttman's procedure checks it directly. Key note: Bogardus's social distance scale is also cumulative in form, but Guttman's method is the one built to test a single dimension, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "The Likert scale (Rensis Likert, 1932) sums agreement ratings on five-point items; it does not test unidimensionality the way Guttman scaling does.", "b": "The Thurstone scale (Louis Thurstone and Ernest Chave, 1929) uses judges to give statements equal-appearing interval values; its hallmark is equal intervals, not a single cumulative dimension.", "d": "The Bogardus scale (Emory Bogardus, 1925) measures social distance towards groups in seven steps; it is the oldest attitude scale and cumulative in form, but it is not the textbook single-trait scale."}}, {"q": "The law of attraction was proposed by:", "concept": "The answer is a: the law of attraction was proposed by Donn Byrne. Working with Don Nelson (1965), Donn Byrne showed that attraction towards a person is a positive linear function of the proportion of similar attitudes that person holds; he set out the full account in The Attraction Paradigm (1971). In the classic \"bogus stranger\" method, students first filled in an attitude questionnaire. Later they saw a questionnaire supposedly filled in by another student, but actually prepared by the experimenter to agree with a chosen proportion of their own answers, and rated how much they would like that stranger. Liking rose steadily with the proportion of agreement, not with the raw number of agreements. Byrne explained this through reinforcement: agreement is rewarding because it confirms our views, and we like people linked with reward (the reinforcement-affect model, developed with Gerald Clore in the 1970s). Option a is right because the similarity-attraction law, attraction rising in a straight line with the proportion of similar attitudes, is Byrne's. If Meera agrees with Riya on 8 of 10 topics but with Arjun on only 3 of 10, the law predicts she will like Riya more. Exam trap: other famous names in attraction research are the decoys: Fritz Heider (1958) gave balance theory, George Homans (1961) social exchange theory, and Leon Festinger (1957) cognitive dissonance, besides the Westgate proximity study. Key note: the option printed \"Humans\" is a misprint for Homans; read as Homans, a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Fritz Heider proposed balance theory (1958), about consistency among a person, another person and an attitude object, and pioneered attribution; the law of attraction is not his.", "c": "George Homans gave social exchange theory (1961), explaining relationships by rewards and costs; he did not state the law of attraction.", "d": "Leon Festinger proposed cognitive dissonance (1957) and social comparison (1954) theories and co-ran the Westgate proximity study (1950), but the law of attraction is Byrne's."}}, {"q": "The origin of the word 'prejudice' can be traced back to the:", "concept": "The answer is b: the word 'prejudice' goes back to a Latin phrase, praejudicium, meaning a judgement made beforehand. Prae means 'before' and judicium means 'judgement', so a prejudice is literally a pre-judgement: deciding about a person before the facts are in. Gordon Allport, in The Nature of Prejudice (1954), traced how the meaning shifted. In classical Latin praejudicium was a precedent, a judgement based on earlier decisions. In English it came to mean a judgement formed before due examination of the facts, a hasty judgement. Finally it took on the emotional flavour of liking or disliking that goes with such an unsupported judgement. Allport's shortest definition was 'thinking ill of others without sufficient warrant'; his fuller one was 'an antipathy based upon a faulty and inflexible generalization'. Social psychology now separates three linked ideas: a stereotype is the belief (cognitive), prejudice is the negative attitude (affective) and discrimination is the unfair action (behavioural). Example: Meera decides that a new classmate from another state is lazy before ever working with her, and keeps that view even after the classmate proves hard working; that fixed prejudgement is prejudice in Allport's sense. Option b is right because the root word, praejudicium, is Latin. Exam trap: Italian descends from Latin, so 'Italian phrase' sounds close, but Italian only has a sister word from the same root; English took 'prejudice' through Old French from the Latin praejudicium, which is the origin every textbook cites.", "traps": {"a": "No Chinese phrase lies behind the word; it entered English from Latin through Old French, so this is a pure distractor.", "c": "Italian has its own word, pregiudizio, but that is a sister word descended from the same Latin root, not the origin of the English 'prejudice'.", "d": "No Sanskrit or other Indian phrase is the source; Allport (1954) traced the word to the Latin praejudicium, a precedent or prior judgement."}}, {"q": "The most preferred research method in experimental psychology is:", "concept": "The answer is d: experimentation is the most preferred method in experimental psychology, because only a true experiment can show cause and effect. In an experiment the researcher deliberately changes one factor, the independent variable (IV), measures its effect on behaviour, the dependent variable (DV), and holds every other factor constant or balances it out (control of extraneous variables). Random assignment of participants to conditions spreads individual differences evenly across groups, so any difference in the DV can be traced to the IV. Wilhelm Wundt (1879) founded the first psychological laboratory at Leipzig on this logic, and the field takes its very name, experimental psychology, from the method. Example: Arjun wants to know whether background music lowers recall. He randomly assigns 40 students to study a word list with or without music, keeps the list, study time and room the same, and compares recall scores. If the music group recalls less, he can conclude that the music caused the drop. Option d is right because experimentation alone combines manipulation, control and random assignment, which together allow a causal conclusion. Exam trap: the correlational method also measures two variables and can show a strong link, so it tempts, but it cannot say which variable causes which or rule out a third variable; descriptive and cross-cultural methods describe and compare without manipulating anything.", "traps": {"a": "The correlational method measures how two variables go together (for example with Pearson's r) without manipulating either, so it shows association, not cause.", "b": "Descriptive methods (surveys, case studies, naturalistic observation) record behaviour as it is; they are useful early in research but give no control and no causal test.", "c": "Cross-cultural research compares people across cultures to test how general a finding is; culture cannot be randomly assigned, so it is a comparative method, not the experimental one."}}, {"q": "The type of fluency which indicates production of new ideas to fit a system or logical theories is called:", "concept": "The answer is b: expressional fluency is the ability to produce new ideas that fit a system, putting words and ideas together into organised phrases, sentences or a logical theory. Joy Paul Guilford (1950, 1967) split creative thinking into divergent production abilities and found that 'fluency', the sheer number of responses a person can produce, is not one ability but several. Word fluency is producing words that meet a structural rule, such as words beginning with 's'. Ideational fluency is producing many ideas that fulfil a requirement, such as uses for a brick or titles for a story. Associational fluency is producing words from a restricted area of meaning, such as synonyms for 'good'. Expressional fluency is fitting ideas into an organised whole: writing sentences from given starting letters, or linking statements into a coherent argument. In Guilford's structure of intellect model it is the divergent production of semantic systems, which is why texts describe it as producing ideas 'to fit a system or logical theories'. Example: given the letters W, C, E and N, Kabir quickly writes 'Why can't everyone nap?' and several more sentences; that skill of arranging ideas into connected form is expressional fluency. Option b is right because only expressional fluency involves organising ideas into a system rather than simply listing them. Exam trap: ideational fluency also concerns 'ideas', so it is the near miss, but it counts the number of separate ideas, not the fitting of ideas into a structured system.", "traps": {"a": "Ideational fluency is Guilford's ability to produce many separate ideas that meet a requirement (uses of a brick); it counts ideas but does not organise them into a system.", "c": "Associational fluency is producing words from a restricted area of meaning, such as synonyms or words related to a given word; it works on relations between words, not systems of ideas.", "d": "Word fluency is producing words that meet a letter or structural rule (words starting with 'pre'), a symbolic skill Louis Thurstone also listed among his primary mental abilities; it involves no system of ideas."}}, {"q": "Who advocated strongly that creativity should not equate with intelligence?", "concept": "The answer is c: Joy Paul Guilford (1950) argued strongly that creativity must not be equated with intelligence. In his presidential address to the American Psychological Association, titled 'Creativity', he complained that psychologists had almost ignored creative ability and that intelligence tests could not find it, because they reward convergent thinking: reasoning towards the one correct answer. Creativity, he said, rests on divergent thinking (later 'divergent production' in his structure of intellect model): producing many varied, original answers to an open question, scored for fluency, flexibility, originality and elaboration. Later work backed the separation. Jacob Getzels and Philip Jackson (1962) found highly creative pupils who were not the highest in IQ, and Michael Wallach and Nathan Kogan (1965) found creativity largely independent of intelligence when children were tested in relaxed, game-like conditions. The threshold hypothesis adds that IQ and creativity go together only up to an IQ of about 120. Example: Riya has an average IQ but lists 25 unusual uses for a newspaper, while her high-IQ friend Ananya lists six ordinary ones; an IQ test alone would miss Riya's talent. Option c is right because Guilford is the theorist who drew the convergent versus divergent distinction and insisted that intelligence tests do not measure creativity. Exam trap: Louis Thurstone (1938) is also a famous name in intelligence theory, but his primary mental abilities, such as verbal comprehension and number, were a multiple-factor account of intelligence, not a case for separating creativity from it.", "traps": {"a": "'Benett' most likely refers to George K. Bennett, lead author of the Differential Aptitude Tests (1947) and the Bennett Mechanical Comprehension Test; his work was aptitude testing, not the creativity and intelligence debate.", "b": "Louis Thurstone (1938) proposed seven primary mental abilities, a multiple-factor theory of intelligence; he is not the theorist known for insisting that creativity differs from intelligence.", "d": "H. J. Butcher reviewed the attempts to separate creativity from intelligence in Human Intelligence: Its Nature and Assessment (1968), but he summarised the debate; Guilford launched and argued it."}}, {"q": "For determining subject's span of attention for digits, letters or figures, the experimenter requires subject to describe what he:", "concept": "The answer is a: in a span of attention experiment the subject describes what he is aware of in one brief glance. The span of attention (span of apprehension) is the number of separate items, such as digits, letters, dots or figures, that a person can grasp in a single momentary act of attention. William Stanley Jevons (1871) threw handfuls of beans into a box and, glancing for an instant, estimated their number: he was never wrong with three or four, but his errors grew beyond that. In the laboratory a tachistoscope flashes a card for a fraction of a second, too short for eye movements, and the subject then states everything he was aware of; the span is the largest number of items grasped correctly. Adults typically take in about six to eight unrelated items, more if the items can be grouped. Example: Kabir sees the letters K, R, T, M, B, S and L flashed for a split second and says he was aware of K, R, T, M and B; his span for letters on that trial is five. Option a is right because the span is defined by awareness: how many items enter consciousness at once, which is exactly what the subject is asked to describe. Exam trap: 'reports' sounds right because the subject does give a verbal report, but the report is only the method; what he reports is what he was aware of. Key note: in practice the subject 'reports' what he 'observed', so c and d are defensible readings of the procedure; but the span is defined by how many items he is aware of at once, and reporting and observing are the method rather than the thing measured, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "'Knows' refers to stored knowledge, which belongs to memory and learning, not to the momentary content of a single act of attention.", "c": "The subject does give a verbal report, but reporting is the procedure used to find out what entered awareness; the span itself is the number of items he was aware of.", "d": "'Observes' describes looking at the display, which every subject does; the span asks how many of the items looked at were actually apprehended in awareness."}}, {"q": "The type of attention where attention is all the time given to the same stimulus which appears and then disappears is called:", "concept": "The answer is a: fluctuation of attention is when attention stays on the same unchanging stimulus, yet the stimulus seems to appear and disappear by turns. Attention is not steady even when we try to hold it on one object: its clearness waxes and wanes in short cycles of a few seconds. Classic demonstrations use a faint stimulus close to threshold. A watch ticking at the edge of hearing seems to fade out and come back. On the Masson disc, a white disc with a broken line of small black marks, the faintest grey ring seen when it spins vanishes and returns. Ambiguous figures such as the Necker cube flip between two views. The stimulus itself never changes, so the coming and going lies in the observer, explained by adaptation of the sense organs and by the rhythmic nature of attention itself. Example: lying in bed at night, Meera listens to the faint ticking of a clock in the next room; for some seconds she hears it clearly, then it seems to stop, then it returns, though the clock never changed. Option a is right because only fluctuation describes attention held on one stimulus that alternately appears and disappears. Exam trap: shift of attention also involves attention moving, but in a shift attention moves to a different stimulus, whereas in fluctuation the stimulus stays the same and only its clearness rises and falls.", "traps": {"b": "Shift of attention means moving attention from one stimulus to another (from the teacher's voice to a noise outside); in the stem attention stays on the same stimulus.", "c": "Span of attention is how many items can be grasped in one brief glance (about six to eight), a measure of the range of attention, not its waxing and waning over time.", "d": "Division of attention is attending to two tasks at once, such as driving while talking; it involves two activities, not one stimulus that comes and goes."}}, {"q": "In naturalistic observation, the experimenter observes the relationship between the independent and the ______ variables.", "concept": "The answer is c: in naturalistic observation the researcher looks at how an independent variable relates to the dependent variable, just as in an experiment, but without manipulating anything. Naturalistic observation means watching and recording behaviour in its usual setting, such as children's play in a school yard or shoppers in a market, without intervening. The researcher cannot assign levels of a variable; instead he notes factors that already differ naturally (age, crowding, time of day) and records the behaviour of interest. The behaviour measured is the dependent variable, and the naturally varying factor plays the part of the independent variable. Because nothing is controlled, a link found this way shows a relationship, not a cause. Jane Goodall's studies of wild chimpanzees at Gombe, begun in 1960, show the method's strength: real behaviour in real settings, with high ecological validity. Example: Riya watches a school playground on crowded and quiet days and counts acts of aggression; crowding is the naturally varying independent variable and the number of aggressive acts is the dependent variable. Option c is right because the variable recorded as the outcome of the other is always the dependent variable, whatever the method. Exam trap: extraneous variables are the uncontrolled factors that worry researchers, and in naturalistic observation they are everywhere, so 'extraneous' tempts; but they are what spoils the relationship, not the second term of it.", "traps": {"a": "A stimulus variable is a type of independent variable (a feature of the environment or task, such as noise level), so it sits on the independent side of the relationship, not the measured side.", "b": "Extraneous variables are other factors that might affect the dependent variable; experiments try to control them and naturalistic observation cannot, but they are not the variable related to the IV.", "d": "Organismic (subject) variables are participant characteristics such as age, sex or intelligence; they are used to group people and so are classed with independent variables, not the measured outcome."}}, {"q": "In the disjunctive reaction time, there are two or more different stimuli presented in a:", "concept": "The answer is b: in disjunctive reaction time, two or more different stimuli are presented in a random order. Reaction time (RT) is the interval between the onset of a stimulus and the start of the response. Franciscus Cornelis Donders (1868) compared three kinds. In simple RT (his 'a' reaction) there is one stimulus and one response, so the subject knows exactly what is coming. In the discrimination or 'c' reaction, several stimuli are shown and the subject responds to only one of them. In the choice or 'b' reaction, several stimuli each have their own response. The last two are disjunctive (compound) reactions, because the subject must first tell which stimulus has appeared. Random order is essential: if the stimuli came in a fixed sequence, the subject would learn it and anticipate, and the task would collapse into simple RT. By subtraction Donders estimated the time taken by mental steps (c minus a gives discrimination time; b minus c gives choice time), founding mental chronometry. Disjunctive RTs are always longer than simple RTs. Example: Arjun must press a key only when a red light appears, not for green or yellow, and the lights come in random order; his RT is longer than when only the red light is ever shown. Option b is right because only a random order prevents anticipation and forces the subject to discriminate. Exam trap: ascending and descending order belong to the method of limits of Gustav Theodor Fechner (1860) in psychophysics, not to reaction time experiments.", "traps": {"a": "A non-random (fixed) order would let the subject learn the sequence and anticipate the next stimulus, removing the discrimination the task is meant to measure.", "c": "Descending order is a feature of the method of limits in psychophysics, where stimulus intensity is lowered step by step to find a threshold; it has nothing to do with presenting different stimuli in RT.", "d": "Ascending order also belongs to the method of limits, where intensity is raised in steps until the subject detects the stimulus; RT experiments do not order stimuli by size."}}, {"q": "The method of minimal changes is also known as the:", "concept": "The answer is b: the method of minimal changes is another name for the method of limits. Gustav Theodor Fechner, in Elements of Psychophysics (1860), set out three classical methods for measuring thresholds. In the method of limits the experimenter changes the stimulus in small steps (minimal changes) in ascending and descending series: a faint tone is made louder step by step until the subject first says 'yes, I hear it', and a clear tone is made softer until he says 'no'. The threshold is the average of these change points. Two constant errors are balanced by alternating the series: the error of habituation (giving the same answer too long) and the error of expectation or anticipation (changing the answer too early). Because the subject's limits are explored in a series of small steps, it is also called the method of serial exploration. The method of constant stimuli presents a fixed set of values many times in random order (the method of right and wrong cases); the method of average error lets the subject adjust the stimulus himself until it matches a standard (the method of adjustment or reproduction). Example: to find Meera's touch threshold, the tester raises the pressure on her skin in tiny steps until she first feels it, then lowers it from above until she no longer does; averaging the two points gives her threshold. Option b is right because 'minimal changes' describes exactly the small serial steps of the method of limits. Exam trap: the differential threshold (just noticeable difference) is a quantity these methods measure, not a method itself.", "traps": {"a": "The method of average error (adjustment or reproduction) lets the subject adjust a variable stimulus to match a standard, as in measuring the Müller-Lyer illusion; the experimenter does not change the stimulus in minimal steps.", "c": "The method of constant stimuli (right and wrong cases) presents a fixed set of stimulus values many times in random order; it is the most accurate but slowest method and does not move in serial steps.", "d": "The differential threshold, or just noticeable difference, is the smallest detectable difference between two stimuli; it is what the methods measure, not one of the methods."}}, {"q": "A characteristic or attribute that can take on a number of values is called a:", "concept": "The answer is c: a characteristic or attribute that can take on a number of values is called a variable. A variable is anything that varies: it differs from person to person, or within the same person across time or conditions. Height, age, anxiety, intelligence, reaction time, gender and social class are all variables, because each can take at least two values. Its opposite is a constant, which has only one value in a given study: if all participants are 20-year-old women, age and sex are constants there. Variables are classified in several ways. By role, the independent variable is the one manipulated or used to group people, and the dependent variable is the measured outcome. By nature, continuous variables can take any value in a range (weight, time), while discrete variables take only separate values (number of siblings). Categorical variables sort people into kinds (religion), and quantitative variables differ in amount (income). Example: Kabir records how many hours each classmate slept last night; because the answer is 5 for one, 7 for another and 8 for a third, sleep duration is a variable. Option c is right because a variable is defined precisely as an attribute that can take different values. Exam trap: 'experiment' is the method that manipulates variables, and 'relations' are what research looks for between variables; both appear in the same chapter, but neither is the attribute that varies.", "traps": {"a": "A virtue is a moral quality of character (honesty, courage), a term from ethics and positive psychology, not the research name for an attribute that takes many values.", "b": "Relations are the links research looks for between variables (for example a correlation between anxiety and test scores); a relation needs at least two variables and is not itself the varying attribute.", "d": "An experiment is the method in which an independent variable is manipulated and its effect on a dependent variable measured; it uses variables but is not one."}}, {"q": "Verbal communication is organized by:", "concept": "The answer is b: verbal communication is organised by language systems. Verbal communication is the sending of messages in words, spoken or written, and words carry meaning only because they belong to a language: a shared, rule-governed system of arbitrary symbols. Every language is organised at several levels: phonemes (the basic sounds), morphemes (the smallest units of meaning, such as 'un-' in 'unhappy'), syntax (rules for combining words into sentences), semantics (meaning) and pragmatics (how language is used in context). Charles Francis Hockett (1960) listed the design features these rules make possible, such as productivity (a small set of sounds yields unlimited new messages) and displacement (talking about things far away in time and place). Verbal communication therefore carries rich cognitive content: facts, ideas, arguments and plans. Non-verbal communication (facial expression, gaze, gesture, posture, tone of voice, touch) works differently: it carries less cognitive content and is the main channel for emotions and attitudes towards others. Example: Ananya tells her friend the exact time and place of a meeting (verbal, language based), while her smile and warm tone tell him she is glad he is coming (non-verbal). Option b is right because the organisation of verbal communication comes from the rules of the language system. Exam trap: options a and c are true descriptions, but of non-verbal communication, so they catch anyone who knows the verbal versus non-verbal contrast but reverses it under time pressure.", "traps": {"a": "Less cognitive content is a mark of non-verbal communication, which conveys feelings and attitudes rather than detailed information; verbal communication carries more cognitive content, not less.", "c": "Expressing emotional states is mainly the work of non-verbal channels (face, tone of voice, posture); it does not describe how verbal messages are organised.", "d": "None of the above is wrong because option b correctly states that verbal communication is organised by language systems."}}, {"q": "Non verbal communication in man is used for managing the immediate:", "concept": "The answer is d: non-verbal communication in humans serves all three uses listed, managing the immediate social situation, supporting verbal communication and replacing verbal communication. The British social psychologist Michael Argyle (1972; Bodily Communication, 1975) described these as the main uses of non-verbal communication (NVC) in human interaction. First, NVC manages the immediate social situation: through facial expression, gaze, posture, distance and touch, people signal emotions and interpersonal attitudes such as liking, dominance or hostility, and so set up and regulate the relationship of the moment. Second, it sustains or supports verbal communication: nods, glances and pauses control turn-taking, give the speaker feedback and add emphasis. Third, it replaces verbal communication when speech is impossible or unwanted, as with gestures across a noisy room or the sign languages of deaf people. He also noted its part in self-presentation and in rituals such as greetings. Example: when Kabir joins a group at a party, their smiles and open posture tell him he is welcome (managing the situation), his friend's nods keep him talking (supporting speech), and a wave from across the room says 'over here' without a word (replacing speech). Option d is right because options a, b and c are, word for word, Argyle's three uses of NVC, so all of them apply. Exam trap: the classic first use, managing the social situation, is often taught alone, so a single option tempts. Key note: as printed, the stem ends 'used for managing the immediate', which only 'social situation' completes, so a can be defended on the literal wording. But the options copy Argyle's three-part list plus 'All of the above', which shows the phrase slipped into the stem from option a; the intended question is what NVC is used for, so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Managing the immediate social situation (signalling emotions and attitudes such as liking or dominance) is Argyle's first use of NVC, but only one of the three; picking it alone leaves out supporting and replacing speech.", "b": "Supporting (sustaining) verbal communication, through nods, gaze and turn-taking signals, is Argyle's second use; it is true but incomplete on its own.", "c": "Replacing verbal communication, as with gestures or sign language when speech is impossible, is Argyle's third use; it is true but incomplete on its own."}}, {"q": "India's population is growing faster than that of:", "concept": "The answer is c: India's population is growing faster than China's. The population growth rate is the yearly percentage increase in a population: births minus deaths (natural increase) plus net migration. China slowed its growth sharply through its one-child policy, in force from 1979 to 2015, so its rate fell to about half a per cent a year by the 2010s, and its population began to shrink in 2022. India never had such a rule, and its rate stayed well above China's, at about 1.2 to 1.3 per cent a year around 2014; the United Nations estimated that India overtook China as the world's most populous country in April 2023. Pakistan, by contrast, has grown faster than India for decades (about 2 per cent a year or more), so India is not growing faster than Pakistan. Environmental and social psychology texts use these figures in chapters on population density and crowding, where rapid growth puts pressure on housing, transport and resources. Example: Arjun's textbook table shows China at about 0.5 per cent growth and India at about 1.2 per cent, so India adds people faster even though China was then the larger country. Option c is right because India's growth rate has been well above China's ever since China's birth-control policies took hold. Exam trap: Pakistan is a neighbour with a similar culture, so it seems a natural comparison, but its growth rate is higher than India's, not lower. Key note: India also grows faster than both North and South Korea, whose rates are well under 1 per cent a year, and on some estimates faster than Indonesia, whose rate has been close to India's; so a, and arguably b, are also literally true. The textbook comparison is between the two population giants, India and China, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Korea (North or South) grows much more slowly than India, well under 1 per cent a year, so the statement is literally true, but the textbook contrast for India's growth is with China.", "b": "Indonesia's growth rate has been close to India's, with estimates differing on which is higher, so it is not the clear comparison; the classic one is China.", "d": "Pakistan's population has grown faster than India's for decades (about 2 per cent a year or more), so India is not growing faster than Pakistan."}}, {"q": "Air pollution has negative effects on our interpersonal interactions and:", "concept": "The answer is a: air pollution has negative effects on our interpersonal interactions and health. Environmental psychology studies how physical surroundings affect behaviour, and air pollution (smog, vehicle exhaust, industrial smoke, carbon monoxide, foul odours and second-hand cigarette smoke) is one of its classic stressors. Its health effects are well known: irritation of the eyes and throat, asthma and other respiratory disease, heart disease, and headaches and dizziness from carbon monoxide. Its social effects are less obvious. Laboratory studies by James Rotton and colleagues (1979) found that moderately foul-smelling air made participants more aggressive towards another person, and unpleasant air in general lowers mood, which in turn makes people more irritable and less pleasant company. Polluted days also keep people indoors and away from others. Example: on a heavy smog day in Delhi, Riya's cough worsens (health) and she snaps at her younger brother over nothing (interpersonal interaction). Option a is right because health is the broad second area, alongside social behaviour, that research on air pollution consistently shows is harmed. Exam trap: heart, mind and emotions are each affected too, so each feels plausible; but they are narrower parts of health or the emotional route to poorer interaction, and the textbook pairing is 'interpersonal interactions and health'.", "traps": {"b": "The mind is affected (carbon monoxide dulls attention and judgement), but 'mind' is not the broad second area that pollution research pairs with social behaviour; that is health.", "c": "Heart disease is one of the health effects of air pollution, so 'heart' is a narrow part of the right answer rather than the broad term.", "d": "Emotions (irritation, low mood) are the route by which pollution harms interpersonal interactions, so they belong to the first half of the stem, not the separate second effect, which is health."}}, {"q": "Personality determines how people react to:", "concept": "The answer is b: personality determines how people react to noise. Noise is unwanted sound, and environmental psychology shows that its effects depend not only on loudness but on the listener. In a study of a college dormitory, Neil Weinstein (1978) measured students' noise sensitivity, a stable personality trait, when they arrived; over the year the noise-sensitive students were more disturbed by dormitory noise, and less able to get used to it, than the less sensitive ones. Other individual differences matter too: introverts, who are more easily aroused, tend to be more bothered by noise than extraverts, and anxious people tolerate it less. Control and predictability also shape reactions. David Glass and Jerome Singer (Urban Stress, 1972) found that people exposed to loud, unpredictable bursts of noise later showed less persistence on puzzles, but these after-effects shrank when people believed they could stop the noise by pressing a button, even if they never pressed it. Example: in the same hostel corridor, Kabir sleeps through loud music while his roommate Arjun, who is highly noise sensitive, lies awake irritated; the noise is the same, their personalities differ. Option b is right because personality traits such as noise sensitivity and introversion are classic, well-studied determinants of reactions to noise. Exam trap: pollution and health risks also draw different reactions from different people, so they seem plausible, but the textbook sentence comes from the noise section. Key note: personality also shapes reactions to pollution and health risks, so the item is loose; it comes from the noise section of the environmental psychology chapter, where personality determines how people react to noise, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Water is not a topic in the research on personality and environmental stress; it is a distractor with no basis in the noise literature.", "c": "Reactions to pollution do vary between people, but the textbook statement about personality determining reactions comes from the noise section, where noise sensitivity and introversion were studied.", "d": "Reactions to health risks are studied in health psychology (risk perception, optimistic bias); they are not the environmental stressor this statement refers to."}}, {"q": "Important properties that make scales of measurement different from one another are:", "concept": "The answer is d: the properties that make scales of measurement different from one another are magnitude, equal intervals and an absolute zero. Stanley Smith Stevens (1946) sorted measurement into four levels, and each level is defined by which of these three properties its numbers have. Magnitude means the numbers show more or less of the attribute (order). Equal intervals means one unit stands for the same amount anywhere on the scale. An absolute (true) zero means zero stands for a complete absence of the attribute. A nominal scale has none of the three: numbers are just labels, like a cricketer's shirt number. An ordinal scale has magnitude only: class ranks show order but not how far apart students are. An interval scale has magnitude and equal intervals but an arbitrary zero: 0 degrees Celsius is not an absence of heat, and IQ scores work the same way, so ratios are meaningless. A ratio scale has all three: weight, reaction time and income, where 40 kg really is twice 20 kg. Example: Meera's class rank (ordinal), the temperature of her classroom in Celsius (interval) and her reaction time in milliseconds (ratio) each tell her teacher progressively more, because each level adds one property. Option d is right because all three properties are needed to separate the four levels. Exam trap: options a, b and c each name a correct pair, so they feel right, but each drops one property: without absolute zero you cannot tell interval from ratio, without equal intervals you cannot tell ordinal from interval, and without magnitude you cannot tell nominal from ordinal.", "traps": {"a": "Magnitude and an absolute zero leave out equal intervals, the property that separates an ordinal scale from an interval scale.", "b": "Magnitude and equal intervals describe an interval scale but leave out the absolute zero that separates interval from ratio scales.", "c": "Equal intervals and an absolute zero leave out magnitude, the first property, which separates ordinal from nominal scales."}}, {"q": "The term stanine comes from:", "concept": "The answer is c: the term stanine comes from 'standard nine'. A stanine is a normalised standard score on a nine-point scale running from 1 to 9, with a mean of 5 and a standard deviation (SD) of about 2. It was developed by the United States Army Air Forces during World War II, partly so that each score would fit in a single column of a punch card. Stanines are assigned by rank: the lowest 4 per cent of the norm group get stanine 1, the next 7 per cent get 2, and so on in the proportions 4, 7, 12, 17, 20, 17, 12, 7 and 4 per cent. Every band except 1 and 9 is half an SD wide; stanine 5 covers the middle 20 per cent, from z = minus 0.25 to plus 0.25. Because each stanine is a broad band, small chance differences in raw score rarely change it, which discourages over-reading tiny gaps between pupils. Related scales are the sten ('standard ten', mean 5.5, SD 2) and the C scale with 11 units. Example: Riya's raw score on an aptitude test falls at the 50th percentile, so she gets stanine 5; Kabir's falls at the 97th percentile, so he gets stanine 9. Option c is right because 'stanine' is a contraction of STAndard NINE. Exam trap: the stanine is a standard score, so option b truly describes what it is, but the question asks where the word comes from, and the word is built from 'standard nine'.", "traps": {"a": "A decile divides a distribution into ten equal parts of 10 per cent each; stanines have nine unequal bands (4 to 20 per cent), so the word does not come from decile.", "b": "A stanine is a kind of normalised standard score, but the word itself is a contraction of 'standard nine', which names its nine units.", "d": "The range is the difference between the highest and lowest scores, a measure of variability; it has nothing to do with the name stanine."}}, {"q": "\"Getting ready to read\" is the developmental task of:", "concept": "The answer is a: 'getting ready to read' is a developmental task of babyhood and early childhood. A developmental task, in the idea of Robert J. Havighurst (Developmental Tasks and Education, 1948), is a task that arises at or about a certain period of life; mastering it brings happiness and success with later tasks, while failure brings unhappiness, social disapproval and later difficulty. Tasks come from physical maturation (walking), cultural pressure (reading) and personal values (choosing a career). Elizabeth Hurlock (1898 to 1988), in Developmental Psychology: A Life-Span Approach, lists Havighurst's tasks for babyhood and early childhood together, roughly birth to six years: learning to walk, to take solid food, to talk and to control elimination; learning sex differences and modesty; getting ready to read; and learning to tell right from wrong and beginning a conscience. Late childhood (about 6 to 12) brings the next set: physical skills for ordinary games, getting along with age-mates, and the fundamental skills of reading, writing and calculating. So getting ready to read belongs to the earlier period, and actually learning to read to the later one. Example: four-year-old Meera listens to stories, turns pages and recognises the letters of her name; she is getting ready to read, long before formal reading lessons in primary school. Option a is right because the task is listed under the combined heading babyhood and early childhood. Exam trap: late childhood is a near miss, because reading itself is learned then, but the stem says getting ready to read. Key note: getting ready to read happens in the early childhood years, so c looks right, but the task list is published under the combined heading 'babyhood and early childhood', so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Late childhood (about 6 to 12) has the task of developing the fundamental skills of reading, writing and calculating; that is learning to read, which follows getting ready to read.", "c": "Early childhood is when getting ready to read actually happens, but Hurlock lists the task under the combined heading babyhood and early childhood, so the single period is the incomplete answer.", "d": "Old age tasks include adjusting to declining strength and health, to retirement and reduced income, and to the death of a spouse; reading readiness has no place there."}}, {"q": "Thirteen to eighteen years is the period of:", "concept": "The answer is b: thirteen to eighteen years is the period of adolescence. Elizabeth Hurlock (1898 to 1988), whose Developmental Psychology: A Life-Span Approach is the standard text in Indian courses, divides life into ten periods: prenatal (conception to birth), infancy (birth to 2 weeks), babyhood (2 weeks to 2 years), early childhood (2 to 6 years), late childhood (6 to 10 or 12 years), puberty or preadolescence (10 or 12 to 13 or 14 years), adolescence (13 or 14 to 18 years), early adulthood (18 to 40), middle age (40 to 60) and old age (60 onward). She further splits adolescence into early adolescence (13 to 16 or 17) and late adolescence (16 or 17 to 18, the age of legal maturity). Adolescence is the transition from childhood to adulthood, marked by the search for identity, growing independence from parents and the rising importance of peers; Granville Stanley Hall (1904) called it a period of 'storm and stress'. Example: Riya at 15 is in early adolescence and preoccupied with how her friends see her; her 11-year-old cousin, just entering the growth spurt, is in puberty; her 25-year-old brother is in early adulthood. Option b is right because Hurlock places adolescence at 13 or 14 to 18 years, which matches the stem. Exam trap: preadolescence (puberty) overlaps the start of the teens, so it tempts, but in Hurlock's scheme it is the short bridging period of about 10 or 12 to 13 or 14 years, which ends as adolescence begins.", "traps": {"a": "Early adulthood in Hurlock's scheme runs from 18 to about 40, beginning exactly where adolescence ends.", "c": "Early childhood is 2 to 6 years, the preschool years, far below the teens.", "d": "Preadolescence (puberty) is about 10 or 12 to 13 or 14 years, a brief overlapping bridge into adolescence, not the whole 13 to 18 span."}}, {"q": "Which one of the following factors does not contribute to interpretation of symptoms?", "concept": "The answer is d: health beliefs are not among the factors that shape how a person interprets a symptom. Shelley E. Taylor, in her textbook Health Psychology (1986), describes how people first notice bodily symptoms and then interpret them before deciding whether to seek care. Interpretation depends on three things. Prior experience: a person who has had a symptom before judges it by what happened last time, so familiar symptoms are often taken less seriously. Expectations: people notice and interpret symptoms they expect, as when someone told that flu is going round reads a sore throat as flu. Seriousness of the symptom: symptoms that cause pain, disrupt daily life or threaten a valued body part such as the eyes or heart are judged serious and acted on quickly. The interpretation then feeds a mental picture of the illness (its identity, cause, consequences, duration and cure), as in the common-sense model of Howard Leventhal (1980). Health beliefs belong to a different idea, the Health Belief Model of Irwin Rosenstock (1966): perceived susceptibility, severity, benefits and barriers, which predict whether a person takes up preventive health behaviour. Example: Arjun had acidity last year, so he dismisses new chest pain as 'gas' (prior experience), though its crushing quality should tell him it is serious. Option d is right because Taylor's factors in interpretation are prior experience, expectations and seriousness, while health beliefs explain preventive behaviour. Exam trap: health beliefs sound as if they should colour interpretation, so d looks like a real factor. Key note: health beliefs do influence what a person does about symptoms, which is why d is tempting to rule out; but Taylor lists prior experience, expectations and seriousness as the factors in interpretation, so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Prior experience is one of Taylor's three factors: having had a symptom before shapes whether it is now seen as harmless or serious.", "b": "Expectations are one of Taylor's factors: people notice and interpret the symptoms they expect, such as after hearing that an infection is going round.", "c": "Seriousness of the symptom (pain, disruption of daily life, threat to a valued body part) is Taylor's third factor in interpretation."}}]});
